WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:06.600
The launch of a new coal mine
in Australia has divided the country.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.480
No more coal!

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.160
No more coal!

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.480
We have to take strong directions,
civil disobedience.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.000
They are over here
pleading with our governments

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.480
not to build new coal mines.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.840
We have a responsibility to them.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.080
An energy giant seeks
to generate electricity in India

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.800
and create jobs across Australia.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
The opportunity is to feed
the very best coal to those plants.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.720
We have an industry
to be super proud of.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.400
Adani is a revolution
for us in central Queensland,

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.320
because it's going to give us
job opportunities.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.400
But some fear the impact
on Australia's natural resources.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.920
Our own government is destroying
the Great Barrier Reef.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.040
We've lost about half of all the corals
of the Great Barrier Reef.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.880
With communities bracing for impact
in their neighbourhoods.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.440
Yes, that's a water bomber.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.440
He's gone back to the airport to refill

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.720
because there's a fire
still going back there.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:09.480
In 30 years,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.520
when we've got raging bush fires,

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.320
our waters running out,
we can't grow crops anymore.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:21.800
Can the Australian government
fight the realities of climate change?

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.000
We're getting more cyclones,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.200
we're getting monsoonal events,

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.360
and climate change is a big issue.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.200
Queensland, Australia.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.520
Suburbs in the south-eastern tip
of the country

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.600
are engulfed in bushfires.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.520
Catastrophic, hot, drought-like conditions

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.240
with wind speeds of 90 miles an hour.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.880
The fires spread fast.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.280
People are evacuated to safety,

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.560
while more than 20 homes
are burnt to ashes.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.400
Rescue services use aerial bombers
to douse the fires,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.720
now spread over 4,200 acres.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.480
An emergency is declared
in vast swathes of Queensland.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:17.560
In May 2019,

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.120
65% of Queensland
was declared to be in drought.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.680
Water supplies for farms and
homes are running dry.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.720
We travel to Stanthorpe,

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.240
a suburban town
two hours from Brisbane,

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.200
to investigate the impact
of this heatwave.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.680
&lt;i&gt;That's James Morris from&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;the New South Wales Rural Fire Service&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.520
&lt;i&gt;giving us the latest update on the...&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.800
The severity of dwindling water supplies

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.840
can be gauged
by the signboards that welcome us.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:06.800
As we arrive, firefighters leave
to douse raging fires nearby.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.480
Stanthorpe's community meeting is called.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.320
Local police and the mayor
have urgent announcements.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.040
I can't stress enough that there are
exclusion zones for safety reasons.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.920
We've got trees that have been burnt out
in those dry roots systems

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.400
that could easily fall over on cars.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.480
There are power lines down.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.760
You could see the direction of winds
that was going,

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.720
and it did with speed
and ferocious flames.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.480
The residents are holed up
inside the centre,

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:46.600
unsure if their homes
have survived the blaze.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.360
While outside,

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.520
water bombing continues.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.720
As fires briefly subside,

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.960
70-year-old Kerry Stratford
is allowed back into her home.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.720
This was all alight on Friday night
when I watched all this burn.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.120
And that house up there
is called the Eagle's Nest.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.600
And I'm surprised that the Eagle's Nest
escaped because it was just burning.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.240
They had to call
the fire inspector yesterday

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:21.920
to put the fire out.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.240
But it got up to there, it stopped there.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.800
So, there must have been
a heap of fire here.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.440
See that brown thing
behind those rocks?

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.600
That's melted. There's a little car.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.320
That's a water tank.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.120
It's melted.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.880
The top of it is melted.
That's how hot the fire was.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.880
Kerry's home miraculously survived.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.200
The fire stopped a few metres
from where she lives.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.280
When you're faced with
not having a home to come back to...

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:52.680
And that's the thing.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:57.160
That's still getting to me,
how lucky I am that my home survived.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.080
I've got my late husband's voice
on the phone,

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.560
that's the only thing
I've got of hearing him talk.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.720
And that was when it snowed here,

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.360
on 17 August, 2015.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:08.720
That's when it snowed.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.400
They evacuated those people out of there.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:14.400
The hardest thing is when
you get the knock on the door.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.480
You know it's coming
from the police saying, "Get out".

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:21.600
And I said, "Oh yeah, I've got
everything packed, everything ready."

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.360
And he said,
"Well, make sure you go."

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:30.280
Stanthorpe's remaining water supply
is being sacrificed for firefighting.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:33.880
Well, the fact that we've been
in drought up here for so long,

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:35.920
we haven't had decent rain for...

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:38.440
a good two years now,

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.320
We've had sporadic rain.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.200
If you go have a look
at Storm King Dam.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:46.280
That'll shock you,

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.320
because that's really shocking out there.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.360
We're on severe water restrictions.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.880
We'll be out of water
at the town here by December.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.160
And I don't know if they're using
the water from our dam out there

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.200
to fill fire bombers for this.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.280
Don't know where they are
getting their water from.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:10.160
The fires have shattered livelihoods,
animal habitats,

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.920
water supplies, and agriculture.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.360
According to
the Climate Council of Australia,

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:21.240
economic loss due to
reduced agricultural productivity

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.840
is projected to exceed
$19 billion by 2030,

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.200
$211 billion by 2050,

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:34.080
and $4 trillion by 2100.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:41.800
Re-using water has now become
the only way to survive the drought

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:43.640
in many parts of the country.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.840
My bath water is still in there,
because I saved that.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:51.320
And then I used my bucket
that I got there in the washing machine.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:52.880
I'll have to get that a rinse there.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:55.160
There's all ashes in there
from the fire,

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:56.800
from the ashes that come through.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:00.200
So, a bucket up with that bucket in there,

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:01.760
wash my clothes in it,

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:03.080
hang it out.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:04.480
Then put it out into the bucket,

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.200
and then it goes on what plants
I've got to keep alive.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:10.360
That's the only way
you water the plants.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:14.000
There's no doubt the climate is evolving,
it is changing.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:17.920
But I know that we're getting
a few degrees warmer each year.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:22.360
We've noticed a big increase
in the global warming,

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:25.440
But I don't know how you stop that.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:29.080
There's something definitely
happening with the Earth.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:35.240
The State of the Climate 2018
report for Australia

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:40.280
reveals a land surface temperature
increase of 1 degree Celsius.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:44.120
That's led to hot, dry conditions,

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:46.440
perfect for bushfires.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.280
The science was saying then
that if we keep putting carbon dioxide

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:53.280
and methane into the atmosphere,

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.040
the temperature will steadily increase.

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:58.240
There will be more frequent extremes,

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:00.000
more very hot days.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.720
There'll be changes to rainfall patterns.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:07.600
The science was saying 30 years ago
that Perth, Adelaide, Melbourne,

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.600
the southern cities of Australia
would get dryer,

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.160
and that's all happened.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:18.840
Already, Australia is ranked
the 7th highest carbon emitter per capita.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:22.160
One big reason, coal-fired power plants.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:27.640
Coal provides fuel for about 70%
of electricity production in Australia.

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:31.640
A majority of these plants are located
in the state of Queensland.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:35.480
The state has
a historical relationship with coal.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:39.000
Mining began here in the 18th century.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:44.640
&lt;i&gt;Behind most coal mining operations&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;lay years of struggle and toil.&lt;/i&gt;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.920
This coal earned royalties
for the Queensland government

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.600
and sparked development
across the country.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.080
But today, the region
is a shadow of its past,

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:02.800
with coal jobs lost to automation.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:07.320
Still, coal production continues to grow

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:09.840
to feed a hungry market abroad.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:16.200
Now, instead of reducing
Australia's carbon footprint,

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:20.480
the Queensland premier has promised
to open new coal mines in her state.

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.880
It's part of Annastacia Palaszczuk's plan
to create thousands of new jobs.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:28.880
But at what price?

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:36.120
Australia is the world's
largest exporter of coal,

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:40.280
a commodity that's aided
the rise in temperatures worldwide.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.480
Coal-fired electricity generation

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:49.240
accounted for 30%
of global CO2 emissions,

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:52.160
according to
the International Energy Agency,

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:56.920
resulting in record temperatures
of 40 degrees Celsius in Australia,

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:00.840
leaving the residents of Stanthorpe
desperate for water.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:12.000
As rescue services
scramble to save lives,

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Queensland's premier calls
for an emergency press conference.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:23.880
So, we have a number
of families at the moment

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:27.520
that are actually going through
some really traumatic times.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:31.360
So, we are in the process
of trying to reach those families.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:33.320
We understand that they're all safe,

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:35.640
but they will be going through

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.440
a lot of grief at the moment,

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.960
and I know that our community
will pull together

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:43.200
and definitely make sure
that they get back on their feet.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:47.480
Premier, we're filming a documentary
from Singapore, from Channel NewsAsia.

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:49.000
Just wanted to get a comment from you

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:53.160
about the increasing number of coal mines
resulting in rising temperatures.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:55.760
Do you feel that this
is a direct impact of that?

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:59.360
Well, we do know that
climate change has a big impact.

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:01.920
We knew that
we are getting more cyclones,

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:04.200
we're getting monsoonal events,

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.280
and climate change is a big issue
that everyone signs up to

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:09.640
in terms of keeping with the Paris treaty.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:15.560
But the latest addition
to Queensland's coal industry

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:19.840
has incensed thousands of anti-coal
protesters across the country.

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:23.600
No more coal!

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:25.520
After the break,

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:28.760
we investigate the politics
of coal in Australia.

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:35.920
Two-thirds of extreme weather events
in the last 20 years

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:38.120
were caused by human activities.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:41.880
The Paris Agreement signed in 2016

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:46.880
aimed to curb emissions to minimise
temperature rise by 1.5 degrees.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.920
If current trends continue,

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:54.680
the world is likely to pass
the 1.5-degree Celsius mark

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:57.560
between 2030 and 2052,

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:01.360
unless it finds a way
to reach net zero emissions.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:05.680
Amid this alarming situation,

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:08.600
the state of Queensland
announced new coal mines

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.640
to boost the economy and create jobs.

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:14.960
That's when Indian billionaire
Gautam Adani

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:17.960
announced plans to build
a mine at Carmichael,

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:20.920
a rural outpost
in the Australian outback.

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.760
At the time, Carmichael was to excavate
60 million tonnes of coal a year.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:29.680
That figure has since been scaled down.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:34.960
In effect, what we are building now
is a 10 million tonne per annum coal mine.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:38.040
We are just one of 125
operating coal mines in Australia.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:41.080
We'll be building 200 kilometres
of greenfield rail construction

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:42.360
tied to the existing rail network.

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:43.600
So combined,

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:47.160
that will be AU$2 billion investment

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:48.440
to be able to get us into production,

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:50.880
and we'll see that coming online in 2021.

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:55.080
The mine would be located
on the Galilee Basin,

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:58.480
160 kilometres north-west of Clermont.

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.240
It would also feature a rail line

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:04.320
connecting the mine
to the Abbot Point terminal.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:08.360
The opening of the Carmichael mine

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:12.040
is expected to attract
more investments in the mining sector,

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:15.440
which activists say would
increase greenhouse emissions

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.280
that trap heat and
make the planet warmer.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:22.720
Roughly 30% of the world's
greenhouse gas emissions

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:27.280
came from electricity production
from coal and gas in 2018.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:30.920
Australia's mining sector,

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:35.320
once the backbone of the economy,
is in a long-protracted slump.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:38.720
I think a lot more businesses
are going to close,

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:40.120
which a lot have already closed.

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:41.040
Haven't they, right?

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.800
They've had to close the doors
because there's no people in the town.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:46.400
People have to move away

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:48.920
because there is nothing here, you know?

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:52.320
The younger generation's got no future.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.480
Yeah, that's right.
The younger generation of Bowen,

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:56.400
they've got nothing.

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:57.680
What did they turn to?

00:14:57.680 --> 00:14:59.920
Look around, the town is dead,

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:02.360
completely, completely dead.

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:06.560
And the town
needs employment real bad.

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:09.240
There's nothing here
for the younger generation.

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:10.800
They all leave town.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:18.000
Adani announced thousands of jobs
to be created over the next few years,

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:21.920
a prospect much welcomed
by the mining community.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:27.160
The pro-Adani movement was
centred around jobs,

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:29.240
jobs for the community.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:32.920
Claremont is a very strong mining town.

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:36.600
I think it was founded on copper mining
back in the 1800s.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.440
And obviously then gold,

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:41.960
and the people of Claremont
following the investment,

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:43.760
and in town,

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:47.400
and the ability for those people
that gained jobs

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:49.640
was very pro-Adani, I think.

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:52.200
I believe that mining is important.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.760
I don't think that
it's sustainable to think

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:57.480
that we can go straight
from coal-fired power

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:01.360
to natural resources like wind or solar,

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.720
but I think they should work
hand in hand.

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:08.080
A number of people locally
work in the mining industry,

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:14.560
so a lot of them are quite keen,
I believe, to get more jobs in that area.

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.840
Unemployment here hovers above 9%,

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:24.720
compared to around 6%
in Queensland as a whole.

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:31.760
That's why Bruce Hedditch
is busy promoting the Adani mine.

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:35.440
He started the Go Adani campaign.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:40.800
It's going to generate a lot of
job opportunities for central Queensland.

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:42.720
The Queensland government last year

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:46.760
received $3.8 billion in coal royalties

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:50.200
from coal mines throughout this state.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:52.320
$3.8 billion.

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:57.960
Next year, the Queensland government
will spend $3.9 billion

00:16:57.960 --> 00:16:59.680
on interest on loans.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:02.720
Now, the opponents of Adani and coal

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:08.320
are asking us, as a community,
to stop coal exports,

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:10.160
stop the world from coming through.

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:11.800
I would love someone to tell me

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:15.000
where the $3.8 billion
is going to come from.

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:18.760
For a town devoid of jobs,

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:23.360
it's a polarising question of
unemployment versus the environment.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:29.000
There are questions about whether or not
the mine will pay appropriate royalties

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:32.040
or any royalties to our state government,

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:35.080
and huge concerns about the fact that

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:37.760
there won't be that many jobs provided,

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:40.000
and that the company has effectively...

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:45.760
massively exaggerated the jobs
that they say it would provide.

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:51.400
Adani's initial promise
of thousands of new jobs

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:53.800
was challenged by a court testimony.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:59.560
An affidavit alleged that Adani Mining's
billion-dollar Carmichael Coal Project

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:03.480
would have 483 full-time
equivalent jobs,

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:09.440
while Queensland as a whole would have
1,206 full-time equivalent jobs.

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:14.000
We'll see over 1,500
direct jobs being created

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:15.640
during the ramp up and construction

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:17.000
for the mine and rail,

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.880
and a further 6,750 indirect jobs.

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:23.440
In total, what we're talking is 8,250 jobs

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:25.920
injected into the Australian economy,

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:27.440
and in particular,
regional Queensland,

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:30.840
and specifically in areas that have
typically high levels of unemployment.

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:31.880
We're talking about areas

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:34.880
that have approached
close to 10% levels of unemployment.

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:36.840
So, the project
is certainly very important

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:38.480
for Australia and for Queensland,

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:40.800
regional Queensland as it relates to jobs.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:47.680
Jobs aside, TV journalist Mark Willacy
has news that's far less hopeful.

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:51.920
He tracked developments
on Adani Mining elsewhere.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:55.280
The company was charged in Zambia

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:58.240
with polluting a major river
and convicted.

00:18:58.560 --> 00:18:59.800
Under Australian law,

00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:04.240
if you have any convictions
or issues involving mining,

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:06.920
and you're in charge,
you have to declare that.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:11.160
That was not declared by this
particular Adani executive at the time.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:14.640
And that coal mine had recently
with Adani being charged,

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:18.680
and it's going to be prosecuted for
supplying false and misleading information

00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:20.960
to the state government
here in Queensland.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:27.560
The State of Queensland imposed
hundreds of environmental conditions

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:29.960
for the Carmichael mine to adhere to.

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:35.520
Particular attention was paid
to Adani's surface water use

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:39.000
and the mine's proximity
to the Great Artesian Basin.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:46.240
The Great Artesian Basin is the biggest
underwater resource in the world,

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:50.160
stretching over 1.7 million
square kilometres.

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:55.360
It is 3,000 metres deep in places,

00:19:55.360 --> 00:20:00.760
and is estimated to contain
65,000 cubic kilometres of groundwater.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:06.480
The basin provides
the only source of fresh water

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:08.760
to much of inland Australia.

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:15.920
Underground coal mines rely on water
to reduce the risk of fires or explosions

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:19.600
by using it to cool the cutting surfaces
of mining equipment,

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:22.320
and prevent coal dust
from catching fire.

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:26.520
So, we know about water
being very precious.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:30.160
The Adani mine would suck up as much water

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:31.360
as the rest of the users

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:32.920
in the whole catchment,

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:35.280
and they don't have to pay as much for it

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:37.800
because our state laws are very lax

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:40.440
and give a whole lot of freebies
to mining companies

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:42.240
for reasons that we can go into later.

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:44.480
Mostly because they make
generous donations

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:45.840
to both sides of politics.

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:48.560
We will not be extracting water
from the Great Artesian Basin.

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:50.840
We don't have any plans,
we don't have any authority,

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:52.600
we don't have any approvals
to extract water

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:54.200
from the Great Artesian Basin.

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:56.360
There are instances where we will
have to extract groundwater

00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:59.600
simply to allow our mining
to be undertaken safely,

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:03.440
so it's simply for safety reasons,
not for water extraction reasons.

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:08.280
And outside of that, all of our water
will be sourced from surface sources.

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:13.760
Tom Cothers,
a former Queensland water chief,

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:16.720
estimates the mine may tap
on a substantial amount

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:18.800
of the state's water resources.

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:23.000
When Adani starts to extract coal

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:26.840
and intercepts
the Great Artesian Basin aquifers,

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:29.920
water will flow out of those aquifers
into the mine pits,

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:33.880
and Adani are allowed to extract
unlimited volumes of water

00:21:33.880 --> 00:21:37.240
out of those mine pits
to keep their mining operations safe.

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:41.720
Adani has modelled an extraction
of water over the life of the mine,

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:43.920
it's 270 gigalitres.

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:48.080
That is equivalent to around
about 55% of Sydney harbour.

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:53.600
Animal graziers like Bruce Currie

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:57.600
rely on the Great Artesian Basin
for their water supplies.

00:21:57.600 --> 00:21:59.640
He lives in Speculation,

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:02.880
a rural outpost in the Australian outback.

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:10.360
People can't exist without water.

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:12.200
If you want to know
what I'm talking about,

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:14.760
turn your water supply off for two days

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:17.200
and try and survive,
regardless of where you are.

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:18.600
Because what's happening here,

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:20.760
they're turning our water off
for perpetuity.

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:22.880
When they destroy it, it's gone forever.

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:26.800
Along with wife Annette,

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:30.480
the Curries have been running
this farmland for generations.

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:34.520
This is the coal seam that they wanted
to get, the 'D' coal seams.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:38.120
This is where we draw our water from,
which is the Clematis Sandstone.

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:41.560
And that is part of the feed
into the Great Artesian Basin.

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:45.440
The only thing is once
they mine that coal seam out,

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:47.720
they have a process
called longwall mining.

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:49.800
And what it does is
once the coal comes out,

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:52.680
they just collapse
all the strata above it.

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:56.560
So, what that'll do,
it will drain that Clematis Sandstone.

00:22:56.560 --> 00:22:59.600
So basically,
it will destroy our water supply,

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:03.520
and it will destroy part of the feed
into the Great Artesian Basin,

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:08.040
and which will impact on the integrity
of the Great Artesian Basin.

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:14.120
Bruce's worries about
destroyed water supplies

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:18.040
inspired him to go on
a fact-finding walkabout overseas.

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:23.720
He wanted to see what the future
of Speculation might look like,

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:25.600
way out in India.

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:28.600
I went to India a few years ago

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:32.280
to actually have a look
at the Adani operation,

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:33.360
and to talk to...

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:35.160
My main concern was
to talk to communities

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:37.360
and farmers over in that area,

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:40.240
and get a good understanding

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:41.640
of the company that would be coming.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:44.240
While I was in India, we went to...

00:23:44.800 --> 00:23:48.680
the Adani special economic zone
up there in the Gulf of Kutch.

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:50.560
And I spoke to fishermen there,

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:57.040
and they highlighted to me the pressures
and the impacts that they are suffering,

00:23:57.040 --> 00:24:02.160
because the Tatas, as well as Adani,
have built a power station there.

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:05.760
They actually released their hot water
out into the ocean,

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:10.520
and which kills the juvenile fish,
fish eggs,

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:13.680
and has a big impact
on the fishing communities

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:17.440
that are trying to survive
on that source of income.

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:22.240
They're also not doing anything about
the fly ash that's coming out of their...

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:24.480
the stacks of their power stations.

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:27.920
And that fly ash is going
across farmer's lands.

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:35.960
Bruce is unsure if similar damage
to the Great Artesian Basin is reversible.

00:24:37.480 --> 00:24:41.080
Once the mine starts
and the de-watering starts,

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:44.960
whether their findings
are accurate or not,

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:49.800
they cannot repair the damage,
and the water is mined forever.

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:51.880
It's lost for perpetuity.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:54.920
Regardless how big their mistake is,
they cannot rectify it.

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:56.360
They cannot correct it.

00:24:56.360 --> 00:24:59.520
And the impact is there for perpetuity.

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:07.680
As the climate gets hotter and drier,
water is even more precious.

00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:14.440
Farmers like Bruce want their groundwater
to be protected from any damage.

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:17.720
It's a shame that
more people aren't aware

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:20.480
of what the Great Artesian Basin
is all about.

00:25:20.480 --> 00:25:25.840
It's one of the biggest underground
fresh water sources in the world.

00:25:25.840 --> 00:25:28.600
And it's not polluted,
it's not contaminated.

00:25:28.600 --> 00:25:30.840
It's environmental terrorism.

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:35.120
The money we've made and the life
we've lived on Speculation...

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:38.680
And to think that they could just...

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:40.640
through the flick of a pen,

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:43.520
take that away from us.

00:25:44.920 --> 00:25:47.680
It's not fair and it's not right.

00:25:47.680 --> 00:25:52.280
Like when you went to India to have
a look at those poor farmers over there,

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:59.400
I don't begin to understand
how that has affected their family.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:01.480
What would we do?

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:06.120
After a prolonged legal battle,

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:11.480
the Adani Carmichael mine has received
the environmental clearances it needs.

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:16.040
But the opposition
to its construction continues.

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:20.680
After the break,

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:25.280
we go underwater to investigate
the impact of rising greenhouse gases

00:26:25.280 --> 00:26:27.400
on the Great Barrier Reef.

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:32.120
There are parts of the Great Barrier Reef

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:34.560
where you can drive on a boat

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:39.040
or swim for kilometres
without seeing a living coral.

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:49.800
Indian energy giant Adani
wants to mine Australian coal,

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:52.360
planning to export it back to India.

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:58.680
Adani is the largest private
thermal power producer in India,

00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:03.120
with an installed capacity
of more than 12,000 megawatts.

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:05.800
But they're ramping up
production in India.

00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:10.960
Adani stands to gain from the rising need
for power in developing South Asia.

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:15.160
To fill the gap in supply,
it's looking at Australian coal.

00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:19.400
The coal will be shipped through
the Abbot Point port operations.

00:27:19.400 --> 00:27:21.320
It's been operating for over 35 years

00:27:21.320 --> 00:27:23.280
in an environmentally responsible way.

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:24.760
There's thousands of ship movements

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:26.800
through the Great Barrier Reef every year.

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:30.520
So, the prospect that the shipment
of our coal through the Great Barrier Reef

00:27:30.520 --> 00:27:33.480
is going to have a direct impact
on the reef is just a falsehood.

00:27:35.360 --> 00:27:36.880
To export this coal,

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:42.720
Adani signed a 99-year lease
with Abbot Point Terminal in 2011.

00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:47.320
The deal cost Adani roughly AU$2 billion.

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:49.560
A few years back, there was a plan

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:52.160
for massive expansion of ports

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:53.360
in the Great Barrier Reef

00:27:53.360 --> 00:27:56.160
and there's basically 6 big ports already.

00:27:56.160 --> 00:27:58.760
And there was a hope by the industry,

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:01.560
with their hand being held by government,

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:05.040
to expand those ports
to ship more coal out,

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:07.080
and more liquefied natural gas out

00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:10.400
through the Great Barrier Reef
to world export markets.

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:13.600
Now, the problem with making ports
bigger and deeper

00:28:13.600 --> 00:28:16.360
is that it requires
a huge amount of dredging.

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:18.440
So, the huge impacts from that dredging

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:22.600
were a very big concern
to marine scientists.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:26.440
The developments surrounding
Abbot Point port

00:28:26.440 --> 00:28:30.240
are being keenly watched
by coral expert Charlie Veron.

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:34.080
That's because of Abbot Point's
close proximity

00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:36.640
to the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park.

00:28:38.360 --> 00:28:42.680
The Great Barrier Reef
is the world's largest coral reef system,

00:28:42.680 --> 00:28:46.720
composed of over 2,900 individual reefs,

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:50.080
large enough that it can be seen
from outer space.

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:54.240
This is the world's
biggest single structure

00:28:54.240 --> 00:28:56.320
made by living organisms.

00:28:58.520 --> 00:29:02.280
Reefs occupy just 1%
of the world's marine environment,

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:05.640
but they provide a home
to a quarter of all marine species,

00:29:05.640 --> 00:29:08.960
including unique fish, turtles and algae.

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:14.080
A large part of the reef is protected
by the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park,

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:17.600
which helps to limit
the impact of human use,

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:19.840
such as fishing and tourism.

00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:26.440
The reef has lost more than half
its coral cover since 1985.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:30.640
High temperatures
cause mass coral bleaching

00:29:30.640 --> 00:29:32.840
due to warm ocean waters.

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:38.160
A coral reef
is a very competitive environment.

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:40.680
Everything competes
with everything else all the time.

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:44.120
So, corals have got
the symbiosis with the algae,

00:29:44.360 --> 00:29:47.960
and that produces food and oxygen.

00:29:48.320 --> 00:29:49.840
But if you raise the temperature

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:55.480
just above the absolute
temperature tolerance the corals have,

00:29:55.480 --> 00:29:58.400
the algae produce too much oxygen,

00:29:58.400 --> 00:30:00.000
and that kills them.

00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:09.760
We head out to investigate
the effects of rising temperatures

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:11.560
on the Great Barrier Reef.

00:30:14.960 --> 00:30:18.200
Charlie Veron is out to collect evidence.

00:30:18.200 --> 00:30:22.520
He's hoping the remaining reef
might be spared from rising temperatures.

00:30:23.440 --> 00:30:27.200
There are parts of the Great Barrier Reef
where you can drive in a boat

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:31.360
or swim for kilometres
without seeing a living coral.

00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:36.280
So, the worst affected
is total death of the whole reef.

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:39.160
And we see that in a few reefs,

00:30:39.760 --> 00:30:43.200
but the majority of reefs
are the Great Barrier,

00:30:43.200 --> 00:30:45.560
have not been wiped out by any means,

00:30:45.560 --> 00:30:48.240
but they have been quite
severely damaged.

00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:54.920
Reefs protect coastlines from flooding
during extreme storms,

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:57.960
which is why saving corals is critical.

00:30:59.400 --> 00:31:03.640
As the seas warm,
and they're warming gradually every year,

00:31:03.640 --> 00:31:09.160
the increase that threatens corals
warms incrementally.

00:31:09.160 --> 00:31:13.200
Just each year,
it gets more and more threatening.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.720
And whether that kills the corals or not

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:20.880
depends on the amount of time
it's exposed to these maximum conditions,

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:22.960
maximum temperature conditions.

00:31:22.960 --> 00:31:26.760
That varies in patches
all over the ocean surface,

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:30.720
but it is increasing year by year,
and that will continue.

00:31:32.560 --> 00:31:34.560
Charlie is now retired,

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:38.200
but has spent his life
raising awareness of coral bleaching.

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:41.120
The other is direct lying,

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:44.280
direct telling of lies by politicians.

00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:46.080
For example,

00:31:47.480 --> 00:31:49.240
politicians say that Australia...

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:51.880
is meeting all its obligations

00:31:51.880 --> 00:31:55.760
under the Kyoto and the Paris Agreement.

00:31:56.360 --> 00:32:00.240
Australia is going absolutely nowhere
near meeting its obligations.

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:04.160
Now, it is not possible for a politician

00:32:04.760 --> 00:32:07.200
to not know the truth,

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:09.520
because scientists know the truth.

00:32:09.520 --> 00:32:11.080
Scientists tell the politicians.

00:32:11.080 --> 00:32:15.120
It's all in publications
of all different levels.

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:18.840
So, if you want to say
we are meeting our obligations

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:22.920
under all these treaties
that have happened,

00:32:24.280 --> 00:32:26.120
you are not telling the truth.

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:28.800
And again, that's political.

00:32:30.600 --> 00:32:34.640
A report card by the government's
Australian Institute of Marine Science

00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:36.160
wasn't encouraging.

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:42.280
Rising water temperatures
are likely to cause more coral bleaching,

00:32:42.280 --> 00:32:45.520
producing irreversible damage to the reef.

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:51.040
Scientists expect the coral may recover
over the next five to ten years,

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:55.920
but only if another mass bleaching event
doesn't occur during that time.

00:32:57.840 --> 00:32:59.280
That's unlikely,

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:02.280
given the current trajectory
of climate change.

00:33:03.640 --> 00:33:05.080
In 2015,

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:08.920
Australia Institute put
the Carmichael mine's expected emissions

00:33:08.920 --> 00:33:11.800
over the next 30 years into perspective.

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:15.720
It believes the annual carbon emissions
of this single coal mine

00:33:15.720 --> 00:33:19.640
would be three times the average
annual emissions from New Delhi,

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:22.880
double those from Tokyo,

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:26.280
six times that of Amsterdam,

00:33:26.920 --> 00:33:29.360
and 20% more than New York City,

00:33:29.360 --> 00:33:30.800
if the mine produces

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:32.800
40 mega tonnes per year.

00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:37.880
Adding more carbon dioxide from mines

00:33:37.880 --> 00:33:42.400
can potentially threaten thousands
of animal species that rely on the reef.

00:33:42.400 --> 00:33:46.320
Not to mention the $6.4 billion
in tourism revenue

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:48.560
and 64,000 full-time jobs

00:33:48.560 --> 00:33:51.560
the Great Barrier Reef
provides to Australia.

00:33:57.000 --> 00:34:01.760
Tourism operator Lindsay Simson
is concerned about her livelihood.

00:34:04.160 --> 00:34:08.600
Her boat tours serve
international tourists on a daily basis.

00:34:09.440 --> 00:34:11.920
If we add more mines to that,

00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:15.880
we're looking at a reef
that's already under terrible threat.

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:20.000
We're looking at 70,000 people
earning their money from tourism,

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:22.920
who won't have anywhere
to take anyone to look at.

00:34:22.920 --> 00:34:25.560
And I mean I've had
laughingly heard people say

00:34:25.560 --> 00:34:27.880
there's something called
disaster tourism.

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:35.200
She decided to investigate Adani
by traveling to India on her own,

00:34:35.200 --> 00:34:37.560
and ended up writing a book.

00:34:39.440 --> 00:34:41.680
He was going to bring jobs to everyone.

00:34:41.680 --> 00:34:44.960
So, he was going to make it
all good again and give lots of work.

00:34:44.960 --> 00:34:50.320
Now, we all know 2%
of Australia's workforce work in mines.

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:52.080
And if you think about that,

00:34:52.080 --> 00:34:54.240
it's an automated industry.

00:34:54.240 --> 00:34:57.000
If we look at how many
of these people are going to get jobs...

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:01.080
Adani promised in India, he was going
to give all these jobs to locals.

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:05.160
He then imported workers from Orissa
and all these other places.

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:06.680
He did not give the local jobs.

00:35:06.680 --> 00:35:08.680
He did for the first year,

00:35:08.680 --> 00:35:11.200
and there is evidence
he's giving work now.

00:35:11.200 --> 00:35:13.320
That work is not going to be there,

00:35:13.320 --> 00:35:16.680
and if you look at it
in terms of long-term work,

00:35:16.680 --> 00:35:20.120
1,300 workers is what
he was talking about,

00:35:20.120 --> 00:35:22.760
versus 70,000 in tourism,

00:35:22.760 --> 00:35:26.600
and those workers
are only going to be "fly-in fly-out".

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:31.720
The coal mine has also sparked
the Native Title Movement.

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:37.880
Every mining project in this country,
it tends to be out in remote country,

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:38.920
and a lot of that country

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:40.600
is traditional aboriginal country.

00:35:40.600 --> 00:35:42.440
And under Australian law,

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:44.720
aboriginal people from those areas

00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:46.920
can claim what they call native title.

00:35:46.920 --> 00:35:50.800
And that's a form of guardianship
over these lands.

00:35:50.800 --> 00:35:53.760
Now, no one has taken
guardianship over that land,

00:35:53.760 --> 00:35:57.440
but there is an aboriginal group there
that wants to do that,

00:35:57.440 --> 00:36:00.440
and they can legitimately
apply to the courts to do that.

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:06.120
As a leader of the Wangan and Jagalingou
native people who owned this land,

00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:09.080
Adrian Burragubba took Adani to court.

00:36:09.720 --> 00:36:10.880
According to him,

00:36:10.880 --> 00:36:14.600
the Carmichael coal mine
can harm around 30,000 hectares

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:17.280
of his community's traditional lands.

00:36:18.320 --> 00:36:22.080
Eventually, the court ruled
in favour of Adani Australia,

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:25.440
who are now seeking
$600,000 in court costs,

00:36:25.440 --> 00:36:27.480
which would bankrupt Burragubba.

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:33.080
Adrian and his clan have now been barred
from entering their own lands.

00:36:33.480 --> 00:36:35.520
This always was aboriginal land.

00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:38.880
It always was first
nation's sovereign land

00:36:38.880 --> 00:36:41.880
because of our culture
and how we maintain our culture.

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:43.320
And we are not going anywhere.

00:36:43.320 --> 00:36:45.080
It's always going to be aboriginal land,

00:36:45.080 --> 00:36:47.240
it's always going to be our land.

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:51.640
And if they want to come in here
and try to move us,

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:53.520
and move our sacred site,

00:36:53.520 --> 00:36:55.680
then they'll be criticised.

00:36:55.680 --> 00:36:59.200
This country will be scrutinised
over the treatment of aboriginal people.

00:36:59.200 --> 00:37:00.760
The Wangan and Jagalingou people

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:04.680
will no longer stand
for this bullying tactic from Adani

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:07.240
or from any government in this country.

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:14.240
But Adrian's resistance sparked
a people's movement across Australia,

00:37:14.240 --> 00:37:16.480
the Stop Adani movement.

00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:19.800
They are over here
pleading with our governments

00:37:19.800 --> 00:37:21.320
not to build new coal mines.

00:37:21.320 --> 00:37:23.440
We have a responsibility to them,

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:27.360
and to our children, for them
to have a safe future to live in.

00:37:34.160 --> 00:37:36.800
The opposition to Adani's Carmichael mine

00:37:36.800 --> 00:37:40.760
has sparked a grassroots
environmental movement across Australia.

00:37:42.200 --> 00:37:44.600
A loose group of environmental activists,

00:37:44.600 --> 00:37:49.920
students and common folk coming together
to pressurise Adani to stop the mine.

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:02.280
Some of them have gathered
at Camp Binbee.

00:38:02.600 --> 00:38:05.520
It's called the Stop Adani movement.

00:38:07.960 --> 00:38:10.480
We've been given
exclusive access to the camp

00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:13.520
after much deliberation
and background checks.

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:24.040
23-year-old Amy Booth
used to live in Victoria,

00:38:24.040 --> 00:38:26.880
but decided to join
the Stop Adani movement

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:28.640
in the Australian outback.

00:38:29.240 --> 00:38:33.040
People are going to be displaced
by the climate crisis.

00:38:33.760 --> 00:38:38.120
I felt that me working in the city,

00:38:38.120 --> 00:38:40.640
sort of doing business as usual,

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:42.560
working nine to five, just...

00:38:43.040 --> 00:38:44.640
isn't where I want to be right now.

00:38:44.640 --> 00:38:48.760
We have a moral obligation to act
and to stop this mine,

00:38:48.760 --> 00:38:51.560
and to stop other
environmental problems going ahead.

00:38:52.840 --> 00:38:57.720
So, I'm willing to put my life
on hold for the moment

00:38:57.720 --> 00:38:59.400
to do what I can

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:02.120
to stop projects like this
from going ahead.

00:39:02.120 --> 00:39:06.080
Because this isn't just about
this particular area.

00:39:06.080 --> 00:39:09.120
Every project that
is a fossil fuel project

00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:12.640
is adding to sea or to the atmosphere,

00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:14.800
and that affects the entire globe.

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:20.400
Protesters like Amy believe that if built,

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.120
Adani's Carmichael mine

00:39:22.120 --> 00:39:26.080
will add billions of tonnes
of carbon pollution to our atmosphere.

00:39:30.240 --> 00:39:33.800
In that context,
it is criminally irresponsible

00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:36.400
to be proposing the opening
of new coal mines

00:39:36.400 --> 00:39:38.720
or new coal-fired power stations.

00:39:38.720 --> 00:39:41.680
And the Adani case
is a particularly important one

00:39:41.680 --> 00:39:44.120
because that would not just be

00:39:44.120 --> 00:39:46.520
a significant coal mine in itself.

00:39:46.520 --> 00:39:51.480
It will potentially open up the whole
Galilee Basin as a new coal province.

00:39:51.480 --> 00:39:56.040
And there are five or six
other possible coal mines in the pipeline

00:39:56.040 --> 00:39:59.480
that would be more likely
to go ahead if Adani started,

00:39:59.480 --> 00:40:03.400
because the infrastructure
for getting the coal to port,

00:40:03.400 --> 00:40:07.720
for managing water, energy
and infrastructure would be there,

00:40:07.720 --> 00:40:11.520
and that would reduce
the start-up costs of other coal mines.

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:15.920
And that's why Adani's become
a really symbolic issue in Australia.

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:23.400
These anti-coal activists are now
taking a more hands-on position

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:27.760
by physically blocking coal trains,
mines and machines,

00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:31.200
a strategy now commonly
known as a lockdown.

00:40:35.800 --> 00:40:38.280
On 22 July 2019,

00:40:38.280 --> 00:40:42.200
protesters set up a blockade
outside the Abbot Point port.

00:40:44.080 --> 00:40:46.600
About 20 members
of the environmental group

00:40:46.600 --> 00:40:50.080
gathered outside
the port entrance at 7am.

00:40:50.160 --> 00:40:51.280
We're ready.

00:40:51.280 --> 00:40:52.320
What are we here for, girls?

00:40:52.320 --> 00:40:54.400
This footage was filmed by Amy,

00:40:54.400 --> 00:40:57.680
as her co-protesters
22-year-old Emily Starr

00:40:57.680 --> 00:40:59.840
and 20-year-old Matilda Heselen

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:02.480
locked themselves to a concrete barrel.

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:08.000
We wanted to start acting

00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:10.520
to show that we weren't going to let...

00:41:10.520 --> 00:41:11.840
We weren't going to sit by

00:41:11.840 --> 00:41:13.440
and let this just happen.

00:41:15.400 --> 00:41:19.760
So, we locked on in terms of,

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:23.000
we had our arms clipped onto this...

00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:29.000
little bar inside a barrel
that was filled with cement

00:41:29.000 --> 00:41:30.640
and then there was a PVC tube

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:33.280
that our arms would go down
and be clipped onto.

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:38.680
They stopped the supply of coal for hours.

00:41:39.680 --> 00:41:42.400
That's when the Queensland police
stepped in.

00:41:43.800 --> 00:41:45.080
As tension mounted,

00:41:45.080 --> 00:41:48.840
a French television crew
filmed the action from the side.

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:53.400
I was willing to be arrested, which I was,

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:57.640
because a short-term inconvenience
of being arrested

00:41:57.640 --> 00:42:00.720
and being given a fine is okay with me,

00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:04.920
because the long-term benefit
of stopping this mine from going ahead

00:42:04.920 --> 00:42:06.400
would be amazing.

00:42:08.720 --> 00:42:11.640
Amy continued filming events that day.

00:42:23.080 --> 00:42:25.320
The journalists and activists are detained

00:42:25.320 --> 00:42:28.400
and charged with
trespassing on the railway line.

00:42:30.880 --> 00:42:35.240
The French crew were later released
after global condemnation grew.

00:42:35.240 --> 00:42:39.000
But they've since been barred
from filming anywhere close to the mine.

00:42:39.320 --> 00:42:40.400
They were embarrassed.

00:42:40.400 --> 00:42:42.120
They think that that was a mistake,

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:43.240
what the officers did up there.

00:42:43.240 --> 00:42:46.160
I know, from the police commissioner down,

00:42:46.160 --> 00:42:48.400
that was why the charges were dropped.

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:50.920
So, it was some overzealous policing

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:53.240
by those police officers up there.

00:42:55.760 --> 00:42:59.760
Emily and Matilda are now facing
a court hearing at Bowen,

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:04.160
uncertain if they'll be allowed
back into Camp Binbee.

00:43:05.080 --> 00:43:08.040
We're hoping that no curve balls
will be thrown when we get in there,

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:11.040
but it'll likely just be sort of...

00:43:11.040 --> 00:43:14.760
Well, we're hoping it'll be a fine
with no restitution order against us,

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:17.520
but we're not sure
what it will be, really.

00:43:17.520 --> 00:43:19.840
Magistrates can do whatever they like.

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:24.680
After an hour,
they come out of the court.

00:43:29.440 --> 00:43:32.040
I felt like he understood
our passion as well,

00:43:32.040 --> 00:43:33.520
which I think was really important.

00:43:33.520 --> 00:43:36.240
I really liked hearing that
he did understand that.

00:43:36.240 --> 00:43:37.800
Even if he didn't necessarily
agree with that.

00:43:37.800 --> 00:43:38.880
Yeah, yeah.

00:43:40.720 --> 00:43:42.760
I think that in Australia,
we have a huge problem,

00:43:42.760 --> 00:43:45.920
and in many places around the world,
we have a huge problem,

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:48.760
where the political systems are broken.

00:43:48.760 --> 00:43:52.600
And that's why we've come
to do these kinds of actions,

00:43:52.600 --> 00:43:55.600
because we feel like
these are the only sort of things

00:43:55.600 --> 00:43:59.280
that can put enough pressure on
political systems to fundamentally change

00:43:59.280 --> 00:44:02.000
rather than just sort of
put a band-aid on the issue.

00:44:03.480 --> 00:44:05.800
We're at a critical point in history.

00:44:05.800 --> 00:44:09.800
There's no doubt that in ecological terms,
we're booked on the Titanic.

00:44:09.800 --> 00:44:11.560
We're heading for the iceberg.

00:44:11.560 --> 00:44:13.760
And there are still irresponsible people

00:44:13.760 --> 00:44:16.840
both literally and metaphorically
throwing coal in the boilers

00:44:16.840 --> 00:44:19.160
as if they wanted to get there faster.

00:44:22.520 --> 00:44:24.480
Coal particulates pollution

00:44:24.480 --> 00:44:29.280
is estimated to shorten some
one million lives annually worldwide.

00:44:30.120 --> 00:44:34.960
One study even estimates premature deaths
arising from coal-related air pollution

00:44:34.960 --> 00:44:38.520
could be as high
as 52,000 people worldwide.

00:44:39.840 --> 00:44:42.920
But with solar and wind power
slowly creeping up,

00:44:42.920 --> 00:44:45.160
there's been positive change.

00:44:47.080 --> 00:44:50.480
Coal's use has been on the decline
in Europe and the US,

00:44:50.480 --> 00:44:52.400
amid cheaper alternatives.

00:44:55.440 --> 00:44:58.120
Adani Australia is promoting solar power

00:44:58.120 --> 00:45:00.880
as a means to bridge
the gap between coal emissions

00:45:00.880 --> 00:45:03.760
and the global push
towards renewable energy.

00:45:04.440 --> 00:45:08.560
Rugby Run, which has an initial capacity
of 65 megawatts,

00:45:08.560 --> 00:45:10.600
is located near Moranbah.

00:45:11.960 --> 00:45:14.040
It's not a question of renewables or coal,

00:45:14.040 --> 00:45:15.600
it is renewables and coal.

00:45:15.600 --> 00:45:16.760
So, from our perspective,

00:45:16.760 --> 00:45:23.120
we've already got around
2,380 megawatts under operation

00:45:23.480 --> 00:45:24.840
in relation to solar.

00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:27.600
We've got another 2,340 megawatts

00:45:27.600 --> 00:45:29.360
currently under construction
of solar capacity.

00:45:29.360 --> 00:45:30.480
So, in short,

00:45:30.760 --> 00:45:35.520
we've got in excess of 4,700 megawatts
of renewables capacity in our portfolio.

00:45:35.520 --> 00:45:38.080
That's a mixture of principally solar,

00:45:38.080 --> 00:45:40.640
but we've also got hybrid in there,
including wind.

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:42.360
We see that, moving forward,

00:45:42.360 --> 00:45:46.040
in order to meet the developing world's
energy demand and requirements,

00:45:46.040 --> 00:45:47.520
it's going to continue to require

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:50.400
underpinning baseload coal-fired
power generation

00:45:50.400 --> 00:45:53.240
in addition to renewables
through the likes of solar, wind,

00:45:53.680 --> 00:45:55.240
hydro and so forth.

00:45:58.200 --> 00:46:01.680
Many believe the solution
to Australia's coal dependence

00:46:01.680 --> 00:46:04.480
has to come
from within the political sphere.

00:46:06.480 --> 00:46:10.520
The Australian Greens
run on four core values:

00:46:10.520 --> 00:46:12.840
ecological sustainability,

00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:14.280
social justice,

00:46:14.280 --> 00:46:16.120
grassroots democracy,

00:46:16.120 --> 00:46:18.160
and peace and non-violence.

00:46:19.960 --> 00:46:21.840
We were just sitting in Parliament,

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:24.800
and the best thing
about that whole experience

00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:27.360
was that we could hear you
more than we could hear them.

00:46:28.400 --> 00:46:30.680
Which is frankly
what a Parliament should be about.

00:46:30.680 --> 00:46:33.320
It should be about
the people's voice being heard.

00:46:34.840 --> 00:46:37.600
The Greens have a plan to phase out coal,

00:46:37.600 --> 00:46:42.280
and create a jobs boom
in the renewable energy export industry.

00:46:42.800 --> 00:46:45.600
We have great economic potential
in clean energy.

00:46:45.600 --> 00:46:48.040
Here in Queensland,
in Australia, globally,

00:46:48.040 --> 00:46:50.120
we know that
there's more jobs in renewables.

00:46:50.120 --> 00:46:52.640
We know that renewable energy
can do the job.

00:46:52.640 --> 00:46:54.880
We've now got the battery storage,
the solar thermal,

00:46:54.880 --> 00:46:58.800
the ways of making clean energy
at 24/7 and dispatchable.

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:01.360
There is no reason
to not go down that path

00:47:01.360 --> 00:47:03.560
and actually safeguard humanity.

00:47:06.200 --> 00:47:10.440
Australia's current bushfire season
has taken 12 lives,

00:47:10.440 --> 00:47:12.880
destroyed millions of hectares of land,

00:47:12.880 --> 00:47:16.200
and wiped out nearly 500 million animals,

00:47:16.200 --> 00:47:20.160
with no signs of rains
or temperatures subsiding.

00:47:22.200 --> 00:47:25.920
Stopping global warming
will require coordinated policies

00:47:25.920 --> 00:47:29.040
by national, state,
and local governments,

00:47:30.200 --> 00:47:33.280
and the development
of climate-friendly energy policies

00:47:33.280 --> 00:47:36.680
that are backed
by politicians and activists alike,

00:47:38.040 --> 00:47:39.720
before time runs out,

00:47:39.720 --> 00:47:42.160
and the irreversible effects
of climate change

00:47:42.160 --> 00:47:45.360
impact the very existence of humankind.

00:47:53.640 --> 00:47:58.120
Brazil's Amazon Rainforest
is being devastated by fires.

00:48:03.440 --> 00:48:07.960
A battle between economic development
and environmental concerns.

00:48:14.880 --> 00:48:18.960
With thousands of miners
invading the forests to hunt for gold.

00:48:25.440 --> 00:48:30.520
Nearly 10,000 square kilometres
of the Amazon are destroyed in 2019.

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:46.600
Displacing hundreds of
indigenous communities

00:48:46.600 --> 00:48:48.560
pushing them to the edge.

00:49:05.920 --> 00:49:10.160
CNA investigates
why the lungs of the world are being lost.

00:49:42.880 --> 00:49:44.520
Sao Paulo, Brazil.

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:48.160
By 3 pm,

00:49:48.160 --> 00:49:51.200
the city is smothered
by black clouds of smoke.

00:49:53.920 --> 00:49:56.720
Scientists eventually discover the cause.

00:50:02.680 --> 00:50:06.440
Fires raging
in the far north of the country

00:50:06.440 --> 00:50:10.440
thousands of miles away
in the Amazon Rainforest.

00:50:12.280 --> 00:50:16.680
Official figures show
more than 87,000 Amazon fires

00:50:16.680 --> 00:50:21.240
are recorded in Brazil
in the first eight months of 2019.

00:50:22.000 --> 00:50:25.600
An increase of 76% from 2018.

00:50:27.320 --> 00:50:32.960
These fires destroy 10,000 sq kilometres
of the Amazon in 2019.

00:50:34.040 --> 00:50:37.000
While the Brazilian government
fails to act...

00:50:40.600 --> 00:50:43.280
the world responds with outrage...

00:50:45.920 --> 00:50:51.440
with criticism focused on newly elected
far-right President Jair Bolsonaro.

00:51:06.880 --> 00:51:08.880
After Bolsonaro's election,

00:51:08.880 --> 00:51:12.760
Brazil's environmental agencies
had their budgets slashed.

00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:17.120
His economic policies
have focused on developing the economy

00:51:17.120 --> 00:51:19.720
inside the Amazon Rainforest.

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:27.200
But Aldem's investigation
unearthed a bigger problem.

00:51:27.760 --> 00:51:31.320
Lack of implementation
of Brazil's forest laws.

00:52:02.480 --> 00:52:05.120
From 1980 to 2019,

00:52:05.120 --> 00:52:10.800
penalties for cutting trees down
in protected areas totalled US$17 billion.

00:52:11.320 --> 00:52:14.360
Less than 4%
of these fines were collected.

00:52:17.320 --> 00:52:19.960
Corruption and the inability
to collect fines

00:52:19.960 --> 00:52:24.080
have encouraged more violators
to set shop inside the Amazon.

00:52:25.200 --> 00:52:28.240
With hundreds and thousands
of dollars to be made.

00:52:57.520 --> 00:53:01.360
The cleared land is used to produce
beef and agri products,

00:53:02.000 --> 00:53:05.400
a large portion of which
is exported to parts of Asia.

00:53:07.040 --> 00:53:11.240
People need to understand that
we are all responsible for this.

00:53:11.280 --> 00:53:14.440
So, even people far away in Pakistan,

00:53:14.440 --> 00:53:17.080
in China, in Australia,

00:53:17.080 --> 00:53:21.920
are also contributing to the
environmental problems in the Amazon,

00:53:21.920 --> 00:53:23.440
because they are consumers.

00:53:23.440 --> 00:53:27.400
They're helping to consume products
that are coming from this region.

00:53:29.240 --> 00:53:30.360
Worldwide,

00:53:30.360 --> 00:53:33.920
forests still cover about
30% of the planet.

00:53:33.920 --> 00:53:36.760
But they are disappearing
at an alarming rate.

00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:40.720
Between 1990 and 2016,

00:53:40.720 --> 00:53:44.520
the world lost 1.3 million
square kilometres of forest,

00:53:44.520 --> 00:53:46.360
according to the World Bank.

00:53:46.360 --> 00:53:49.360
That's an area larger than South Africa.

00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:28.000
We arrive in the state of Para

00:54:28.000 --> 00:54:31.560
to investigate the effects
of the fires in northern Brazil.

00:54:33.440 --> 00:54:36.960
The burnt down jungles
now resemble a war zone.

00:54:42.920 --> 00:54:45.760
Erika Berenguer
is an environmental scientist,

00:54:45.760 --> 00:54:48.680
who's lived in the Amazon
for the last 10 years,

00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:52.000
studying the destructive force
of the fires here.

00:55:35.880 --> 00:55:39.320
Erika takes us for a walk
inside a protected area

00:55:39.960 --> 00:55:43.000
to show us the impact of fires
on the Amazon.

00:56:06.800 --> 00:56:12.120
The Amazon Rainforest has
410 billion trees in this region alone,

00:56:13.120 --> 00:56:17.000
with a total of 76 billion tons
of carbon stored in them.

00:56:55.360 --> 00:56:57.560
Animals are being affected too.

00:56:58.280 --> 00:57:01.080
It's believed that there are about
three million different species

00:57:01.080 --> 00:57:03.680
of plant and animal life in the Amazon.

00:57:34.600 --> 00:57:39.440
But President Bolsonaro wants to abolish
protection for indigenous lands.

00:57:40.200 --> 00:57:42.280
His calls have been echoed by miners,

00:57:42.280 --> 00:57:46.680
who've put self-preservation
ahead of environmental conservation.

00:57:50.120 --> 00:57:51.320
After the break,

00:57:51.320 --> 00:57:54.520
we meet these miners
who have invaded the Amazon

00:57:54.520 --> 00:57:56.000
in search of gold.

00:58:02.200 --> 00:58:05.440
Brazil is one the biggest
gold producers in the world.

00:58:10.240 --> 00:58:14.680
With output reported
at 81 million kilograms in 2018.

00:58:15.960 --> 00:58:18.840
But there is a new gold rush
in Brazil today,

00:58:20.200 --> 00:58:25.720
one fuelled by soaring unemployment
in regions inside the Amazon Rainforest.

00:58:27.640 --> 00:58:30.920
They're invading public lands,
logging the trees,

00:58:30.920 --> 00:58:34.080
doing some gold mining
and damaging these areas,

00:58:34.080 --> 00:58:36.280
or doing some fishing and hunting.

00:58:38.360 --> 00:58:42.760
As the planet loses
the Amazon Rainforest at a rapid pace,

00:58:42.760 --> 00:58:45.480
there are miners
on the other end of the equation

00:58:45.480 --> 00:58:47.920
who are making increasing gains.

00:58:48.520 --> 00:58:50.800
Locally known as garimpeiros,

00:58:50.800 --> 00:58:55.000
their rising incomes developed cities
like Peixoto de Azevedo.

00:58:56.680 --> 00:58:59.160
Located in Mato Grosso state,

00:58:59.160 --> 00:59:03.040
they are gold producing hubs
for Brazil's lucrative gold industry.

00:59:07.640 --> 00:59:10.560
Today, miners have become more organised,

00:59:10.560 --> 00:59:13.960
creating cooperatives
to hunt for gold collectively.

00:59:18.600 --> 00:59:21.000
One such cooperative is called Coogavepe

00:59:21.000 --> 00:59:26.400
that was founded in 2008 by
former Sao Paulo banker Gilson Camboim.

00:59:32.160 --> 00:59:35.120
He takes us for a tour
of the Coogavepe's gold mines

00:59:35.120 --> 00:59:37.040
that are under his supervision.

01:00:02.600 --> 01:00:04.560
Such gold mines are legal

01:00:04.560 --> 01:00:08.800
as part of the Brazilian government's
strategy to monetise the Amazon.

01:00:14.720 --> 01:00:18.240
But drone images reveal
the damage to the environment.

01:00:21.480 --> 01:00:24.160
This was once a lush green forest

01:00:24.160 --> 01:00:27.600
with flora and fauna
spread all across the landscape.

01:00:28.880 --> 01:00:32.080
It's been replaced by
energy guzzling machines.

01:01:01.720 --> 01:01:05.920
Coogavepe alone has deforested
3,000 square kilometres

01:01:05.920 --> 01:01:09.240
of Amazon Rainforest since 2008.

01:01:11.080 --> 01:01:15.720
Gold mining in the Amazon Rainforest
is attractive for the high yield of gold.

01:01:17.520 --> 01:01:18.440
On average,

01:01:18.440 --> 01:01:23.480
the concentration of gold
is 0.5 gram for every one ton of soil.

01:01:25.360 --> 01:01:28.880
Inside the Amazon,
the yield is much higher.

01:02:05.680 --> 01:02:09.720
Gold is found underground
after the topsoil is removed.

01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:13.160
Once a 10-metre ditch is ready,

01:02:13.160 --> 01:02:18.280
the miners at Coogavepe,
villagers from rural Brazil, get to work.

01:02:47.760 --> 01:02:52.040
A hydraulic machine is parked near
the edge of this vast mining pit

01:02:52.040 --> 01:02:55.440
that has been carved into
the rainforest's reddish-brown earth.

01:02:57.680 --> 01:03:00.720
Hundreds of gallons of mud sludge
produced by the miners

01:03:00.720 --> 01:03:02.680
is passed over carpets...

01:03:04.080 --> 01:03:06.640
with the hope that
gold flakes stick to it.

01:03:07.840 --> 01:03:12.800
The carpet is then washed into drums
and the mud is taken to the final process.

01:03:18.880 --> 01:03:20.760
After extensive panning,

01:03:21.640 --> 01:03:24.360
mercury is used to extract gold
from the mud

01:03:24.360 --> 01:03:26.720
through a process
called amalgamation.

01:04:11.040 --> 01:04:14.760
The crew at Coogavepe
work without protective equipment

01:04:15.840 --> 01:04:19.480
or procedures to help them
discard the mercury safely.

01:04:20.120 --> 01:04:24.320
The amalgam of mercury and gold
goes through the final process.

01:04:46.720 --> 01:04:49.360
The heat applied evaporates the mercury,

01:04:49.360 --> 01:04:51.560
and the gold is left in the pan.

01:04:58.160 --> 01:05:01.560
Coogavepe mined 400 grams of gold today.

01:05:03.240 --> 01:05:06.720
This is distributed equally
amongst everyone at the mine.

01:05:07.280 --> 01:05:10.160
It will sell for US$500.

01:05:11.760 --> 01:05:13.320
In the poor countryside,

01:05:13.320 --> 01:05:16.120
that is a fortune
for a day of hard labour.

01:06:15.640 --> 01:06:18.640
The raw gold is sold to shops in the city,

01:06:18.640 --> 01:06:21.120
who process it into gold bars.

01:06:22.280 --> 01:06:25.400
The final product is exported
to countries across Asia,

01:06:25.400 --> 01:06:27.200
the Middle East and America.

01:06:27.560 --> 01:06:30.640
Earning billions of dollars
in revenue for the government.

01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:34.520
Mines like Coogavepe are legal.

01:06:34.520 --> 01:06:39.360
And all part of President Bolsonaro's
master plan to monetise the Amazon.

01:07:06.720 --> 01:07:08.680
But what about the Amazons?

01:07:10.200 --> 01:07:11.320
On the ground,

01:07:11.320 --> 01:07:14.840
the damage done by legal mines
is irreversible.

01:07:42.120 --> 01:07:43.000
By law,

01:07:43.040 --> 01:07:47.440
Coogavepe is supposed to restore
the forests once the mine is closed.

01:07:49.400 --> 01:07:52.560
So Gilson takes us
to a restored part of the mine.

01:07:53.920 --> 01:07:56.680
He's turned one area into a fish farm

01:07:57.480 --> 01:07:59.800
while planting trees in another.

01:09:03.760 --> 01:09:06.760
As greed takes over
concern for the environment,

01:09:06.760 --> 01:09:09.200
many miners are going for the big bucks,

01:09:09.200 --> 01:09:11.360
by starting their own mines.

01:09:11.360 --> 01:09:15.560
Deep inside protected territories,
away from the authorities.

01:09:16.720 --> 01:09:20.080
After the break,
we dig deep into illegal mining,

01:09:20.080 --> 01:09:22.880
and meet the people
whose lands have been invaded

01:09:22.880 --> 01:09:25.320
in the protected forests of Brazil.

01:09:29.600 --> 01:09:31.920
Brazil's Amazon Rainforest

01:09:31.920 --> 01:09:35.760
is one of the planet's
best defences against climate change.

01:09:36.480 --> 01:09:39.840
It absorbs billions of tons
of carbon dioxide.

01:09:41.280 --> 01:09:43.760
But deforestation for logging, mining

01:09:43.760 --> 01:09:47.320
and for farming crops
and livestock has devastated it.

01:09:51.640 --> 01:09:53.200
In the last 10 years,

01:09:53.200 --> 01:09:58.640
the rainforest has lost an area
the size of 8.4 million soccer fields.

01:10:00.920 --> 01:10:04.000
But the mining companies
want to use indigenous lands,

01:10:04.000 --> 01:10:06.160
to expand operations,

01:10:06.160 --> 01:10:08.280
and make more profits.

01:10:10.280 --> 01:10:13.200
They are pushing
for a change in Brazilian law

01:10:13.200 --> 01:10:16.280
allowing firms to mine
inside protected lands

01:10:16.280 --> 01:10:19.080
and indigenous reserves
across the nation.

01:10:53.280 --> 01:10:55.680
Despite having protected status,

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:59.440
indigenous territories
are already facing an invasion.

01:11:02.280 --> 01:11:04.000
In Para state alone,

01:11:04.000 --> 01:11:08.520
Brazilian authorities estimate
that 30 metric tons of illegal gold

01:11:08.520 --> 01:11:12.800
worth about US$1.1 billion
has been excavated.

01:11:13.480 --> 01:11:17.840
That's six times more than the legal
amount of gold traded in Brazil.

01:11:39.240 --> 01:11:43.280
This mining boom could lead to the
extinction of indigenous communities

01:11:43.280 --> 01:11:47.360
that have weathered centuries
of calamities in Brazil's rainforests.

01:11:49.200 --> 01:11:52.640
Roughly 900,000 indigenous people
live in Brazil,

01:11:52.640 --> 01:11:55.880
less than 0.5% of the population.

01:12:02.120 --> 01:12:07.560
They belong to 400 tribes
and speak more than 270 languages.

01:12:12.160 --> 01:12:15.800
On such tribe facing illegal mining
in their protected areas

01:12:15.800 --> 01:12:17.280
are the Munduruku.

01:12:19.800 --> 01:12:23.000
We travel 16 hours to the city of Bubure.

01:12:32.640 --> 01:12:34.920
We are on the Tapajos River in Para state

01:12:34.920 --> 01:12:37.200
and we're heading to the Munduruku lands,

01:12:37.200 --> 01:12:40.240
indigenous protected lands
by the Brazilian government

01:12:40.240 --> 01:12:43.280
that have been invaded
by illegal gold miners

01:12:43.280 --> 01:12:45.760
and we're going to meet
the chief of the tribe

01:12:45.760 --> 01:12:48.360
to understand the extent of the problem.

01:12:58.400 --> 01:13:01.400
Juarez is the Chief
of the Munduruku Tribe.

01:13:03.280 --> 01:13:05.840
He leads nearly 14,000 members,

01:13:05.840 --> 01:13:08.720
who have splintered
into dozens of small villages,

01:13:08.720 --> 01:13:15.080
scattered across a territory
slightly larger than 3,360 soccer fields.

01:13:15.760 --> 01:13:18.480
They have lived here
for thousands of years.

01:13:55.680 --> 01:13:58.360
That's why Juarez has to create borders

01:13:58.360 --> 01:14:00.960
or more commonly known as demarcation

01:14:00.960 --> 01:14:04.160
to identify his tribe's indigenous lands.

01:14:05.080 --> 01:14:08.720
Motivated by the surging
international price of gold,

01:14:08.720 --> 01:14:14.080
miners choose to prospect for gold
in protected reserves to evade taxes.

01:14:14.520 --> 01:14:18.000
It's estimated that nearly
50,000 illegal miners

01:14:18.000 --> 01:14:20.800
are working inside Para state alone.

01:15:39.720 --> 01:15:43.360
Permanently evicting indigenous people
from their land is forbidden

01:15:43.360 --> 01:15:47.160
under article 231
of Brazil's Constitution.

01:15:49.920 --> 01:15:53.880
But the Bolsonaro government is planning
to build dams and infrastructure

01:15:53.880 --> 01:15:57.240
in the Tapajos River basin
to boost the economy.

01:16:00.440 --> 01:16:01.960
Armed with machetes,

01:16:01.960 --> 01:16:07.080
Juarez and his crew ensure
the conservation of 178,000 hectares

01:16:07.120 --> 01:16:09.120
of Amazon rainforest.

01:16:41.520 --> 01:16:44.800
Juarez takes us to one
such illegal mining site,

01:16:44.800 --> 01:16:47.680
an hour's boat ride on the Tapajos River.

01:16:53.320 --> 01:16:54.520
As we arrive,

01:16:54.520 --> 01:16:57.000
we spot a mining transport truck.

01:16:58.960 --> 01:17:02.240
We tread carefully
as most of the miners are armed

01:17:02.240 --> 01:17:04.320
and do not like visitors.

01:17:06.720 --> 01:17:09.240
Miners in protected areas such as this

01:17:09.240 --> 01:17:12.640
excavate land deep inside the forests,

01:17:12.640 --> 01:17:16.920
away from the authorities
keeping clear of any inspections.

01:18:00.480 --> 01:18:03.320
As we reach an abandoned mining pit,

01:18:03.800 --> 01:18:08.280
the extent of the devastation
can only be gauged from high above.

01:18:15.720 --> 01:18:20.880
Kilometres of protected Amazon Rainforest
are now mere ditches.

01:19:18.120 --> 01:19:19.920
We don't venture too far,

01:19:19.920 --> 01:19:23.760
we've been warned
that the illegal miners may open fire.

01:19:24.960 --> 01:19:28.840
Five tribal leaders have already
lost their lives in this battle.

01:19:29.640 --> 01:19:32.080
But with little enforcement of the law,

01:19:32.080 --> 01:19:34.240
the destruction continues.

01:20:14.680 --> 01:20:16.960
A map published by an NGO

01:20:16.960 --> 01:20:21.480
shows illegal mining sites
in 37 indigenous territories.

01:20:22.440 --> 01:20:26.280
In total,
nearly 2,300 illegal mining sites

01:20:26.280 --> 01:20:30.640
with sophisticated infrastructure
have been found in protected areas.

01:20:33.960 --> 01:20:37.440
Juarez fears for his life
and his community.

01:20:37.920 --> 01:20:39.280
Currently, in Brazil,

01:20:39.280 --> 01:20:43.160
mining on indigenous territories
is totally prohibited.

01:20:45.240 --> 01:20:50.840
The Amazon contains about 20% of
the world's total volume of river water.

01:20:51.320 --> 01:20:56.280
More than 80% of the world's food
has its origins in this rainforest.

01:20:57.120 --> 01:20:59.800
Apart from tearing down native forests,

01:20:59.800 --> 01:21:03.360
miners have also started
digging for gold in the rivers,

01:21:03.360 --> 01:21:06.480
contaminating these waterways
with mercury.

01:21:30.600 --> 01:21:33.200
Dredging for gold disrupts rivers,

01:21:33.200 --> 01:21:37.760
toxic pollutants seep into plants,
animals and people.

01:22:28.480 --> 01:22:32.240
Juarez and his community
can no longer fish in the river,

01:22:32.240 --> 01:22:34.440
they have to hunt for food on land.

01:22:34.920 --> 01:22:39.280
Researchers found 92% of all hair
and fish samples collected here

01:22:39.280 --> 01:22:41.120
had high levels of mercury,

01:22:41.120 --> 01:22:45.480
resulting in abnormalities
in the central nervous system of infants.

01:22:47.920 --> 01:22:51.600
So, Juarez has decided
to take things into his own hands.

01:22:52.840 --> 01:22:53.960
After the break,

01:22:53.960 --> 01:22:58.240
we follow the Munduruku
as they plan to invade Brazil's Congress,

01:22:58.240 --> 01:23:01.240
to pressure politicians to take action.

01:23:22.320 --> 01:23:25.160
Brazil's President Jair Bolsonaro

01:23:25.160 --> 01:23:29.240
plans to reduce indigenous territories
in the Amazon Rainforest.

01:23:33.000 --> 01:23:36.960
The tribes are being sacrificed
to help boost the economy

01:23:36.960 --> 01:23:38.600
to create jobs.

01:23:39.080 --> 01:23:42.000
The mining sector is encroaching
into the rainforest

01:23:42.000 --> 01:23:44.120
and the hunt for gold has begun.

01:23:46.880 --> 01:23:48.280
In 2018,

01:23:48.280 --> 01:23:52.240
Brazil's gold export increased 150%,

01:23:52.240 --> 01:23:54.800
on an output of 95 tons.

01:23:56.800 --> 01:23:58.240
With demand rising,

01:23:58.240 --> 01:24:01.880
miners are pushing deeper
inside protected forests,

01:24:02.200 --> 01:24:05.240
radically changing
the landscape forever.

01:24:10.200 --> 01:24:15.600
This is why indigenous tribes have decided
to invade the federal capital, Brasilia.

01:24:16.800 --> 01:24:18.200
Alessandra Korap,

01:24:18.200 --> 01:24:22.080
a Munduruku indigenous activist
is leading the charge.

01:24:35.120 --> 01:24:37.320
Alessandra has arrived in Brasilia,

01:24:37.320 --> 01:24:40.720
the country's capital,
to meet her Munduruku clan.

01:24:45.280 --> 01:24:48.520
A local school's been turned
into a makeshift camp.

01:24:53.440 --> 01:24:59.440
These 50 Munduruku tribals plan to visit
Brazil's Congress to meet politicians.

01:25:00.160 --> 01:25:05.360
And demand that their lands,
protected under Brazil law, be left alone.

01:25:48.720 --> 01:25:52.240
According to Brazil's National Institute
for Space Research,

01:25:52.240 --> 01:25:54.240
in 2018 alone,

01:25:54.240 --> 01:25:58.880
five square kilometres of indigenous
Munduruku land was deforested.

01:25:59.360 --> 01:26:00.840
In 2019,

01:26:00.840 --> 01:26:04.720
the tree massacre increased
to 15 square kilometres.

01:26:05.880 --> 01:26:10.240
Alessandra believes illegal miners
have the backing of politicians.

01:26:53.800 --> 01:26:57.160
Next day, the tribe prepares for battle,

01:26:57.160 --> 01:27:01.680
They believe Bolsonaro's rhetoric
has encouraged the advance of the miners

01:27:01.680 --> 01:27:04.280
deeper into indigenous territory.

01:27:23.600 --> 01:27:27.440
The Munduruku are on their way
to Congress to block a bill

01:27:27.440 --> 01:27:32.640
that will allow mining companies to
operate inside indigenous lands legally.

01:27:34.120 --> 01:27:37.440
It's a key campaign promise
of President Bolsonaro.

01:27:37.440 --> 01:27:42.280
If approved, indigenous tribes and
their way of life may be gone forever.

01:27:47.800 --> 01:27:49.840
An economic recession in Brazil

01:27:49.840 --> 01:27:53.480
has driven large numbers of
unemployed villagers into the jungle

01:27:53.480 --> 01:27:54.880
to hunt for gold.

01:27:55.960 --> 01:27:59.360
The price is being paid
by indigenous people.

01:28:03.640 --> 01:28:06.360
The Munduruku arrive at Brazil's Congress,

01:28:06.360 --> 01:28:09.080
the main legislative body of the country.

01:28:12.120 --> 01:28:16.280
This movement is helped by the country's
only indigenous congresswoman,

01:28:16.280 --> 01:28:18.160
Joenia Wapichana.

01:28:20.160 --> 01:28:24.960
She's only the second indigenous woman
to have ever been elected to Congress.

01:29:07.000 --> 01:29:10.680
Of more than
594 congress representatives,

01:29:10.680 --> 01:29:12.480
only three show up.

01:29:18.160 --> 01:29:23.040
The others celebrate a football team's
participation in a local league.

01:29:36.280 --> 01:29:37.760
In a separate chamber,

01:29:37.760 --> 01:29:42.200
Alessandra makes an impassioned plea
for the right to the Munduruku.

01:30:29.080 --> 01:30:33.000
As Brazil is polarised on the issue
of mining in the Amazon,

01:30:33.480 --> 01:30:36.640
other industries
are coming up with solutions.

01:30:42.080 --> 01:30:43.840
Laurent started Pecsa,

01:30:43.840 --> 01:30:47.000
a cattle ranching firm in June 2015.

01:30:48.280 --> 01:30:52.160
He helps Brazilian farmers
with sustainable cattle farming.

01:30:56.520 --> 01:30:58.600
According to the Amazon Institute,

01:30:58.600 --> 01:31:03.400
some 12 million hectares of cattle field
have been degraded in the Amazon region.

01:31:05.200 --> 01:31:07.360
To create new grazing land,

01:31:07.360 --> 01:31:10.320
vast expenses of the Amazon are cleared.

01:31:11.560 --> 01:31:15.760
To date, an estimated 70%
of deforested Amazon

01:31:15.760 --> 01:31:18.400
has been converted into pasture land.

01:31:18.920 --> 01:31:21.440
But Pecsa offers a solution.

01:31:23.280 --> 01:31:26.200
So, here we use rotated grazing.

01:31:26.200 --> 01:31:29.920
Rotated grazing means that
we have a production model

01:31:29.920 --> 01:31:32.600
that has several divisions.

01:31:32.600 --> 01:31:37.560
And the cattle will stay in one division
for just a few days

01:31:37.560 --> 01:31:40.960
and then go to the other one
and then to the other one.

01:31:40.960 --> 01:31:45.520
And while the cattle do this cycle,

01:31:45.520 --> 01:31:48.160
the first one is resting.

01:31:48.600 --> 01:31:50.160
This allows...

01:31:50.560 --> 01:31:53.360
First, the fact that the cattle

01:31:53.360 --> 01:31:57.040
always has access
to the best part of the plant,

01:31:57.040 --> 01:31:58.160
only the leaves.

01:31:58.160 --> 01:32:03.640
Doing an efficient production,
we don't need to clear any more land.

01:32:03.640 --> 01:32:08.080
Many big corporations over the world

01:32:08.080 --> 01:32:11.720
have made commitments to remove

01:32:11.720 --> 01:32:14.360
deforestation from the supply chains.

01:32:16.000 --> 01:32:18.480
From 2010 to 2017,

01:32:18.480 --> 01:32:24.360
Brazil's beef exports
climbed 25% to 1.5 million tons,

01:32:24.360 --> 01:32:26.920
with Hong Kong importing the most.

01:32:28.680 --> 01:32:30.800
To accommodate global beef demand,

01:32:30.800 --> 01:32:33.280
cattle ranchers
are driving their herds deeper

01:32:33.280 --> 01:32:35.400
into the Amazon Rainforest.

01:32:36.880 --> 01:32:39.840
But since operations began
in Alta Floresta,

01:32:39.840 --> 01:32:43.880
Pecsa has restored 10,000 hectares
of pasture land.

01:32:45.520 --> 01:32:49.520
It now runs 20 farms that
sustainably manage cattle,

01:32:49.520 --> 01:32:53.160
34,000 in 2017 alone.

01:32:55.440 --> 01:32:56.440
For Laurent,

01:32:56.440 --> 01:33:00.680
reinventing cattle feed
was another necessity of the hour.

01:33:00.680 --> 01:33:02.840
Methane emitted by livestock

01:33:02.840 --> 01:33:07.840
accounts for about 5.5%
of worldwide greenhouse gasses.

01:33:08.720 --> 01:33:12.200
Greenhouse gasses will increase
global temperatures.

01:33:13.160 --> 01:33:15.080
When they have this poor diet,

01:33:15.080 --> 01:33:18.320
only based on pasture
and low-quality pasture

01:33:18.320 --> 01:33:20.840
they produce more methane.

01:33:20.840 --> 01:33:24.120
When we do the reform of the pastures,

01:33:24.120 --> 01:33:27.600
and then we introduce this supplement,

01:33:28.120 --> 01:33:31.160
we change the diet of the animals,

01:33:31.200 --> 01:33:32.880
so we have two benefits.

01:33:32.880 --> 01:33:38.440
One is to reduce by more or less 40%

01:33:38.440 --> 01:33:41.240
the amount of methane
that is emitted by the animals.

01:33:41.240 --> 01:33:45.120
So, when you consider
the methane of the animals

01:33:45.120 --> 01:33:47.120
and also the carbon from the soil,

01:33:47.120 --> 01:33:51.600
altogether, the emissions
are reduced by 90%.

01:33:57.080 --> 01:34:00.240
Today, the deforestation
in the Amazon

01:34:00.240 --> 01:34:04.200
claims an area of the size
of two football fields a minute.

01:34:04.560 --> 01:34:07.240
If consumption patterns don't change,

01:34:07.280 --> 01:34:11.520
50% of the Amazon may vanish by 2030,

01:34:11.520 --> 01:34:15.360
destroying one of the world's
largest carbon sinks.

01:34:15.360 --> 01:34:19.640
Protecting existing rainforests
and aggressively planting new ones,

01:34:19.640 --> 01:34:21.800
may be the only way forward.

01:34:21.800 --> 01:34:26.120
And perhaps one of the last few ways
to help save this planet,

01:34:26.120 --> 01:34:28.440
and all who call it home.

01:35:09.960 --> 01:35:17.960
Pakistan's Himalayan glaciers are melting
at the fastest rate in human history.

01:35:19.160 --> 01:35:24.120
Nearly 36% of its ice sheet
will be gone by 2100.

01:35:36.000 --> 01:35:38.360
In a battle for water in Kashmir,

01:35:38.360 --> 01:35:41.480
a nuclear flashpoint
between India and Pakistan.

01:35:52.720 --> 01:35:54.920
We want justice!

01:35:54.920 --> 01:35:59.680
Impacting the water needs of
more than 2 billion people in South Asia.

01:36:00.680 --> 01:36:04.600
With rising heatwaves in mega cities
increasing water consumption,

01:36:04.600 --> 01:36:07.440
communities are fighting over
dwindling supplies.

01:36:08.280 --> 01:36:12.200
So basically, we're inside
illegal theft area.

01:36:25.960 --> 01:36:28.200
I don't think many Pakistanis
know how bad it is.

01:36:29.720 --> 01:36:34.400
Could an ambitious tree-planting drive
save the melting glaciers?

01:36:34.440 --> 01:36:38.720
So we want to restore
1,000,000 hectares in Pakistan.

01:37:22.320 --> 01:37:23.520
Karachi...

01:37:23.920 --> 01:37:28.760
Pakistan's largest city
with a population of 20 million.

01:37:28.800 --> 01:37:33.360
The economic nerve centre of the country
is brought to a standstill.

01:37:38.440 --> 01:37:43.320
A severe heat wave strikes
the southern port city in June 2015,

01:37:44.280 --> 01:37:48.200
with temperatures
as high as 49 degrees Celsius.

01:37:50.000 --> 01:37:52.960
The blazing sun causes total mayhem.

01:37:53.400 --> 01:37:55.040
Unaware and unprepared,

01:37:55.040 --> 01:38:00.800
more than 2,000 people across Karachi
perish from dehydration and heat stroke.

01:38:04.000 --> 01:38:07.640
Hospitals are ill equipped
to deal with such a calamity.

01:38:08.520 --> 01:38:12.600
The Abbasi Shaheed hospital mortuary
runs out of space.

01:38:15.000 --> 01:38:19.680
As a result, mass graves are dug
to dispose off the bodies.

01:38:28.480 --> 01:38:33.040
Memories of 2015 have come back
to haunt Karachi today.

01:38:34.560 --> 01:38:39.200
In 2019, the heatwave has returned
with average daytime temperatures

01:38:39.200 --> 01:38:41.320
hitting 49 degrees.

01:38:42.680 --> 01:38:46.400
The Sindh government
declares a city-wide emergency.

01:38:47.840 --> 01:38:51.640
We head out to the Abbasi Shaheed
Hospital's heat stroke unit.

01:38:57.040 --> 01:39:00.680
To our surprise,
there are no heatstroke patients today.

01:39:52.680 --> 01:39:56.560
Karachi government officials
are also better prepared.

01:39:56.560 --> 01:39:59.400
Mobile units equipped with water sprays,

01:39:59.400 --> 01:40:03.280
are deployed strategically
during the hotter hours of the day.

01:40:07.280 --> 01:40:12.240
Despite the 49-degree temperature today,
no casualties are reported.

01:40:21.880 --> 01:40:24.800
This is the new normal in Karachi.

01:40:25.640 --> 01:40:29.560
An international climate report
puts Pakistan at number six

01:40:29.560 --> 01:40:33.840
on the list of top 10 countries
most affected by climate change.

01:40:37.920 --> 01:40:41.160
And in mega cities like Karachi,
lack of trees,

01:40:41.160 --> 01:40:46.280
miles of asphalt roads and tall buildings
have increased heat absorption.

01:40:47.960 --> 01:40:51.800
Making matters worse
is an unchecked growing population

01:40:51.800 --> 01:40:55.600
in poorly planned
yet densely populated settlements.

01:40:58.360 --> 01:41:04.000
Karachi is a spectacular example
of population explosion.

01:41:04.000 --> 01:41:08.320
It was a small coastal city
which became the capital city

01:41:08.320 --> 01:41:11.000
and it is now the largest city.

01:41:11.000 --> 01:41:12.640
So in terms of population,

01:41:12.640 --> 01:41:15.200
you have more people,
you need more water.

01:41:15.200 --> 01:41:18.280
You have more shops and factories.

01:41:18.280 --> 01:41:20.240
Also, mismanagement.

01:41:20.960 --> 01:41:25.320
Karachi did not manage
to protect its water courses.

01:41:27.400 --> 01:41:28.960
With rising temperatures,

01:41:28.960 --> 01:41:33.040
the massive population of Karachi
consumes more water.

01:41:33.800 --> 01:41:37.200
Karachi needs 1.1 billion gallons daily

01:41:37.200 --> 01:41:40.800
to supply water
to roughly 20 million residents.

01:41:41.160 --> 01:41:44.320
But the Water Board,
the city's water distributor,

01:41:44.320 --> 01:41:48.760
is only able to provide
550 million gallons per day.

01:41:51.040 --> 01:41:54.560
Mohammad Arif is a resident of Korangi.

01:41:54.560 --> 01:41:57.000
His tap runs dry.

01:42:20.920 --> 01:42:25.960
Broken water distribution lines
multiply the problem for Karachiites.

01:42:27.400 --> 01:42:28.720
At 2am,

01:42:28.720 --> 01:42:32.720
Arif arrives at the main distribution
pipeline of the government,

01:42:32.720 --> 01:42:34.840
a hundred metres from his home.

01:42:36.320 --> 01:42:38.720
He plans to divert water to his home

01:42:38.720 --> 01:42:41.800
with the aid of hoses
and a hand-held motor.

01:43:06.000 --> 01:43:10.440
There are nearly a million people
in this neighbourhood of Korangi alone.

01:43:11.040 --> 01:43:12.640
And everyone's thirsty.

01:43:36.000 --> 01:43:40.720
So what does the Water Board,
Karachi's water distributor have to say?

01:44:15.000 --> 01:44:18.000
As water becomes rare
and difficult to access,

01:44:18.000 --> 01:44:20.240
a black market is thriving.

01:44:21.640 --> 01:44:24.480
According to the Karachi Water
and Sewerage Board,

01:44:24.480 --> 01:44:30.000
42% of water is lost
in distribution before reaching consumers.

01:44:30.400 --> 01:44:33.080
So where does all this water go?

01:44:35.640 --> 01:44:40.360
We go in search of Karachi's water thieves
for an answer to this question.

01:44:45.040 --> 01:44:46.840
After much deliberation,

01:44:46.840 --> 01:44:51.560
we finally convince a water thief
to take us to his theft point.

01:44:51.560 --> 01:44:53.680
We film the process secretly.

01:45:26.520 --> 01:45:31.320
So basically, we are inside
the illegal theft area

01:45:31.320 --> 01:45:33.920
where the water is stolen in Karachi.

01:45:35.960 --> 01:45:39.160
The water thief takes us
to a congested slum.

01:45:42.160 --> 01:45:46.680
In the meantime, the mastermind arrives
to explain how it works.

01:47:42.720 --> 01:47:45.680
He takes us to where
he steals the water from.

01:47:46.280 --> 01:47:49.760
Water that he then sells
at exorbitant rates,

01:47:49.760 --> 01:47:52.800
while government officials get their cut.

01:48:23.040 --> 01:48:25.720
Theft, corruption, and disrepair

01:48:25.720 --> 01:48:29.920
hamper water access for millions
in Pakistan's largest city.

01:48:30.480 --> 01:48:34.760
It costs millions of dollars in losses
to the national exchequer.

01:48:36.680 --> 01:48:42.160
This stolen water is diverted from
the poor to the rich who can pay more.

01:48:42.560 --> 01:48:45.960
A short-term solution the city developed
for its water needs

01:48:46.000 --> 01:48:47.840
were water tankers

01:48:47.840 --> 01:48:51.480
that now control
much of Karachi's water distribution.

01:48:53.240 --> 01:48:58.080
More than 10,000 tankers operate
in the city under government control,

01:48:58.080 --> 01:49:03.920
making an estimated 50,000 delivery trips
to consumers across Karachi each day.

01:49:05.560 --> 01:49:07.520
But with few checks and balances,

01:49:07.520 --> 01:49:11.440
excessive bribes kick in
when heatwaves hit Karachi,

01:49:11.960 --> 01:49:15.480
allowing tanker drivers
to jack up rates as they will.

01:49:19.520 --> 01:49:22.240
Delivery of a 1,000-gallon water tanker

01:49:22.240 --> 01:49:25.960
now costs between $12 and $18 per trip.

01:49:26.520 --> 01:49:31.440
This is a steep price,
especially for larger, poorer families

01:49:31.440 --> 01:49:35.080
who spend a third of their income
on stolen water.

01:49:36.600 --> 01:49:39.920
A global study projects
that nearly five billion people

01:49:39.920 --> 01:49:43.240
will live in water-stressed
regions by 2050,

01:49:43.680 --> 01:49:46.880
while the United Nations estimates
that water shortages

01:49:46.880 --> 01:49:51.720
can displace hundreds of millions
of people as early as 2030.

01:49:53.440 --> 01:49:57.000
Water is a massive political issue
in Karachi

01:49:57.000 --> 01:49:59.680
and corruption is
in the middle of it all,

01:50:01.080 --> 01:50:03.960
with one political party
depriving the water needs

01:50:03.960 --> 01:50:06.440
of another party's constituency.

01:50:49.680 --> 01:50:54.480
While solving Karachi's water crisis
seems like a distant pipe dream,

01:50:54.480 --> 01:50:59.840
the residents of Karachi are left with
no option but to buy stolen water.

01:51:02.240 --> 01:51:03.320
After the break,

01:51:03.320 --> 01:51:06.120
we embed with the
Karachi Water Task Force

01:51:06.120 --> 01:51:08.840
in their efforts to stop water theft.

01:51:31.280 --> 01:51:32.440
Karachi.

01:51:32.760 --> 01:51:36.160
Pakistan's largest city has an epidemic.

01:51:36.560 --> 01:51:40.240
To capitalise on climate change
related water shortages,

01:51:40.240 --> 01:51:43.560
Karachi's black market
for water is thriving.

01:51:45.320 --> 01:51:48.680
Millions of dollars' worth of
government water is being stolen

01:51:48.680 --> 01:51:51.720
and sold to people
who can pay a premium.

01:51:51.720 --> 01:51:55.240
So the Karachi Water Board
has stepped up their efforts.

01:51:57.120 --> 01:52:02.520
Chief Security Officer Tabish Raza
heads the task force against water theft.

01:52:58.600 --> 01:53:00.600
As soon as we reach the location,

01:53:00.600 --> 01:53:04.000
the thieves are spotted
and the raid begins.

01:54:28.360 --> 01:54:31.400
A water motor is confiscated
in the raid

01:54:31.720 --> 01:54:34.960
as Tabish explains the kind of money
changing hands

01:54:34.960 --> 01:54:37.800
in Karachi's illegal water industry.

01:55:15.480 --> 01:55:19.160
Tabish has received another tip-off
from a neighbouring town.

01:55:19.520 --> 01:55:23.400
A textile factory is stealing
and reselling water.

01:56:19.160 --> 01:56:23.280
The illegal pump from the bore
is taken out and confiscated.

01:56:28.680 --> 01:56:32.320
This is a daily routine
for Tabish and his team.

01:56:32.320 --> 01:56:36.400
They arrest the perpetrators
and destroy 10 pumps a day.

01:56:37.560 --> 01:56:41.320
But in a city of 20 million people,
and with limited resources,

01:56:41.320 --> 01:56:45.400
the Karachi Water Board
can only play catch-up with the thieves.

01:56:46.680 --> 01:56:51.000
And climate change will only worsen
Karachi's water woes.

01:56:52.160 --> 01:56:55.280
Climate change will mean more floods,

01:56:55.280 --> 01:56:57.400
it will mean more droughts,

01:56:57.720 --> 01:57:03.440
it will mean heatwaves like the one
we saw in Karachi 2 years ago,

01:57:03.440 --> 01:57:06.280
it will mean reduction in the quantity

01:57:06.280 --> 01:57:09.760
and reduction in the quality of water.

01:57:09.760 --> 01:57:11.200
Both are serious.

01:57:11.200 --> 01:57:13.640
There will be significant health impacts.

01:57:15.360 --> 01:57:19.080
Soaring temperatures in South Asia
are reaching peak levels

01:57:19.080 --> 01:57:21.920
due to rising greenhouse gas emissions.

01:57:21.920 --> 01:57:25.360
The heat is melting the water wall
of South Asia

01:57:25.360 --> 01:57:29.840
the Himalayas, that supply water
to the rest of the subcontinent.

01:57:31.960 --> 01:57:36.640
The glaciers in northern Pakistan
in the disputed region of Kashmir

01:57:36.640 --> 01:57:40.360
are the biggest ice sheets
outside the polar regions.

01:57:40.360 --> 01:57:42.880
These ice sheets are melting.

01:57:44.480 --> 01:57:47.880
We drive 15 hours
from Islamabad to Gulmit,

01:57:47.880 --> 01:57:52.400
a village in northern Pakistan
to investigate its impact.

01:57:55.920 --> 01:57:57.800
According to a new study,

01:57:57.800 --> 01:58:03.120
36% of the glaciers found in the region
will be gone by 2100.

01:58:03.720 --> 01:58:08.960
Sitara Parveen is a glaciologist who has
studied these ice sheets for a decade.

01:58:10.240 --> 01:58:15.120
This was once an area which had
plenty of water from this glacier

01:58:15.120 --> 01:58:18.160
because the glacier was much higher,

01:58:18.160 --> 01:58:21.200
and even up to the levels
of these moraines.

01:58:21.200 --> 01:58:22.240
You can see here.

01:58:22.240 --> 01:58:25.840
This moraine shows
the level was much higher.

01:58:25.840 --> 01:58:29.640
So, the water was recharging

01:58:29.640 --> 01:58:32.160
these irrigation channels.

01:58:32.160 --> 01:58:35.320
The glaciers are very sensitive
to climate.

01:58:35.320 --> 01:58:38.480
They react according to
the temperature and precipitation.

01:58:38.520 --> 01:58:41.600
When it is hot, it melts a lot.

01:58:42.840 --> 01:58:44.640
Since 1988,

01:58:44.640 --> 01:58:49.760
the snow line in northern Pakistan
has receded by 1.1 kilometres.

01:58:50.240 --> 01:58:55.040
What scientists and glaciologists
thought wasn't possible in 1,000 years

01:58:55.040 --> 01:58:57.400
has happened within 30.

01:58:58.240 --> 01:59:01.520
If global emissions stay
at current levels,

01:59:01.520 --> 01:59:05.520
future generations
may never see ice in South Asia.

01:59:08.880 --> 01:59:13.520
These glaciers are also important
for these farmers who are living here,

01:59:13.520 --> 01:59:17.240
who depend on agriculture
for their livelihood.

01:59:17.240 --> 01:59:23.240
Because you can see that
this is a very moisture-deficit area,

01:59:23.240 --> 01:59:26.040
less rainfall in these plain areas

01:59:26.040 --> 01:59:30.160
where the people practice agriculture.

01:59:30.160 --> 01:59:35.160
So, it is not possible without
the water from these glaciers.

01:59:36.400 --> 01:59:41.280
Nestled in the Karakoram mountains
as high as 23,000 feet,

01:59:41.280 --> 01:59:44.400
village communities like these
are vulnerable.

01:59:44.720 --> 01:59:47.200
&lt;i&gt;Welcome, welcome, welcome

01:59:47.200 --> 01:59:53.640
&lt;i&gt;We welcome you today...

01:59:53.640 --> 01:59:56.880
Lakes forming within the glaciers
often burst

01:59:56.880 --> 02:00:01.360
causing flash floods,
destroying life and property downstream.

02:00:07.680 --> 02:00:08.600
Shaukat Ali,

02:00:08.600 --> 02:00:12.920
a local resident and tour guide
takes us to the Ghulkin Glacier.

02:00:13.960 --> 02:00:15.480
Due to pollution,

02:00:15.480 --> 02:00:20.040
fine particles of "black carbon"
are deposited onto white glaciers...

02:00:20.040 --> 02:00:22.600
speeding up the melting process.

02:00:58.480 --> 02:01:00.920
After a three-hour trek over ice,

02:01:00.920 --> 02:01:03.160
we arrive at the Ghulkin Glacier,

02:01:03.160 --> 02:01:05.640
one of the largest in northern Pakistan.

02:01:06.920 --> 02:01:10.160
Streams of water run down
into the crevasses

02:01:10.840 --> 02:01:13.320
collecting at the bottom of the glacier.

02:01:16.200 --> 02:01:19.040
As the sun blazes onto the ice sheet,

02:01:20.560 --> 02:01:22.000
Shaukat points out,

02:01:22.000 --> 02:01:25.440
this water collects into a massive lake
within the glacier

02:01:25.440 --> 02:01:29.720
that can burst anytime
and flood the village downstream.

02:02:01.640 --> 02:02:03.440
In June 2019,

02:02:03.440 --> 02:02:06.800
a glacial lake formed
in the neighbouring Shisper Glacier

02:02:06.800 --> 02:02:09.360
that forced the government to react.

02:02:09.360 --> 02:02:12.320
Local officials hurriedly built
a concrete wall

02:02:12.320 --> 02:02:15.240
to prevent the water
from bursting the banks.

02:02:15.920 --> 02:02:19.760
But they stood no chance
in front of the savage wall of water

02:02:19.760 --> 02:02:22.200
that came hurtling down at the village.

02:02:23.040 --> 02:02:25.960
Tariq remembers that fateful day.

02:03:09.000 --> 02:03:12.720
Tariq believes more than 30 homes
were washed away,

02:03:12.720 --> 02:03:16.440
and if proper planning was done,
they could have been saved.

02:03:46.320 --> 02:03:48.960
Tariq takes us to the Shisper Glacier.

02:03:48.960 --> 02:03:52.320
He believes
it's the next disaster in the making.

02:03:54.640 --> 02:03:59.240
This black glacier is physically
moving 5-6 metres a day

02:03:59.240 --> 02:04:03.440
downstream to the village
due to rising temperatures.

02:04:03.440 --> 02:04:08.920
And local communities are concerned
if it's going to destroy homes again.

02:04:38.160 --> 02:04:43.000
The region is expected to face
mass water shortages in around 50 years

02:04:43.000 --> 02:04:45.280
when glaciers become too small.

02:04:48.760 --> 02:04:49.960
After the break,

02:04:49.960 --> 02:04:53.280
we take you to the highest
militarised zone in the world,

02:04:54.440 --> 02:04:59.760
where India and Pakistan
are battling a war on top of a glacier.

02:05:05.840 --> 02:05:10.200
Pakistan has been bestowed with five
of the biggest rivers in the world.

02:05:10.800 --> 02:05:14.440
The mighty Indus, Jhelum, Chenab,

02:05:14.440 --> 02:05:19.000
Ravi and Sutlej that have cascaded down
the plains of the Punjab

02:05:19.000 --> 02:05:20.720
for hundreds of years.

02:05:22.360 --> 02:05:26.400
These rivers are formed by
melting glaciers in the Karakoram

02:05:26.400 --> 02:05:28.240
and Himalayan ranges.

02:05:30.720 --> 02:05:34.680
One such glacier is nestled between
India and Pakistan,

02:05:34.680 --> 02:05:37.920
in the eastern Karakoram range
in the Himalayas.

02:05:38.480 --> 02:05:42.200
It's called the Siachen Glacier
and is claimed, in full,

02:05:42.200 --> 02:05:44.600
by both Pakistan and India.

02:05:46.440 --> 02:05:52.040
It's been disputed territory
after the two countries split in 1947.

02:05:52.360 --> 02:05:57.320
The contended area is a sheet of ice
nearly 1,000 square miles in total.

02:05:58.920 --> 02:06:02.000
Unable to settle the land dispute
diplomatically,

02:06:02.000 --> 02:06:05.400
troops were amassed on top
of the glacier by both countries.

02:06:06.840 --> 02:06:10.160
Pakistan and India
have spent millions of dollars a month

02:06:10.160 --> 02:06:12.160
to keep troops posted here.

02:06:18.360 --> 02:06:23.760
At 6,700 metres, it is the highest
militarised zone in the world.

02:06:25.680 --> 02:06:30.600
The dispute over land between India
and Pakistan transcends downstream,

02:06:30.920 --> 02:06:34.960
to the waters that continue to flow
from India to Pakistan.

02:06:36.440 --> 02:06:39.000
After partition in 1947,

02:06:39.000 --> 02:06:43.000
India and Pakistan
were unsure of how to share water.

02:06:43.000 --> 02:06:46.920
Hence, the Indus Waters Treaty
was signed in 1960

02:06:46.920 --> 02:06:50.560
between then Indian Prime Minister
Jawaharlal Nehru

02:06:50.560 --> 02:06:54.160
and Pakistani President Ayub Khan
in Karachi.

02:06:56.560 --> 02:06:58.120
Brokered by the World Bank,

02:06:58.120 --> 02:07:01.480
the treaty allowed both countries
to use the water available

02:07:01.480 --> 02:07:03.600
in the Indus System of Rivers.

02:07:04.720 --> 02:07:10.280
The two countries, due to
historical problems and animosity,

02:07:10.280 --> 02:07:12.640
could not agree on joint management,

02:07:12.640 --> 02:07:16.640
so it was considered a second partition

02:07:17.360 --> 02:07:18.960
should take place.

02:07:19.840 --> 02:07:23.280
Pakistan got the rights to use
the three western rivers,

02:07:23.280 --> 02:07:27.080
the Indus, Jhelum
and Chenab exclusively,

02:07:27.080 --> 02:07:30.480
while India won the rights
to use the three eastern rivers,

02:07:30.480 --> 02:07:33.440
the Ravi, Sutlej and Beas.

02:07:36.000 --> 02:07:39.720
But today, tensions between
the nuclear neighbours over Kashmir

02:07:39.720 --> 02:07:42.600
is putting pressure on this water treaty.

02:07:42.600 --> 02:07:47.840
The Hindu nationalist BJP led by
Prime Minister Modi has rattled things.

02:07:47.840 --> 02:07:52.480
Modi now wants to build dams
to block the flow of water into Pakistan,

02:07:52.880 --> 02:07:58.240
making the Indus river, a hotspot
that might escalate into a nuclear war.

02:08:03.440 --> 02:08:05.840
I think the next fight
would be over the water.

02:08:05.840 --> 02:08:07.680
The world is going to fight over water.

02:08:07.680 --> 02:08:09.360
And we never realised.

02:08:09.360 --> 02:08:12.360
And this Indus Waters Treaty,
when it was done in 1970s

02:08:12.360 --> 02:08:14.880
that was the time that
we could have solved the problem.

02:08:14.880 --> 02:08:17.400
But India is always more authoritative,

02:08:17.440 --> 02:08:19.760
they never obey the rules

02:08:19.760 --> 02:08:21.720
even if they are international

02:08:21.720 --> 02:08:24.920
so this treaty remained,
what would I say...

02:08:24.920 --> 02:08:27.160
It remained just in the books.

02:08:28.120 --> 02:08:30.280
Pakistan's semi-arid climate

02:08:30.280 --> 02:08:34.600
means more than 90% of harvests
depend on irrigation.

02:08:36.880 --> 02:08:40.920
You are going to have Pakistanis
receiving less water

02:08:41.880 --> 02:08:45.480
so when you receive less water,
you blame the supplier.

02:08:45.480 --> 02:08:47.360
There is a potential...

02:08:48.840 --> 02:08:50.000
source of conflict.

02:08:50.000 --> 02:08:54.160
And the Secretary-General made a report
to the UN Secretary-General.

02:08:54.160 --> 02:08:57.760
Made a specific reference
to India and Pakistan

02:08:57.760 --> 02:09:03.480
and said that climate change
is likely to put pressure

02:09:03.960 --> 02:09:08.920
on the Indus Waters Treaty
which has served the two countries

02:09:09.280 --> 02:09:13.400
for almost 60 years.

02:09:18.800 --> 02:09:21.080
Despite four full-blown wars,

02:09:21.080 --> 02:09:24.720
India and Pakistan
have never had a water war

02:09:25.080 --> 02:09:29.640
but the situation in the disputed region
of Kashmir is escalating.

02:09:37.320 --> 02:09:42.720
A majority of Pakistan's rivers originate
from Indian-administered Kashmir.

02:09:42.720 --> 02:09:47.000
And in August 2019,
India did the unthinkable.

02:09:48.240 --> 02:09:52.400
India's dams released nearly
6 million litres of water per second

02:09:52.400 --> 02:09:55.280
into Pakistan without intimation.

02:09:56.200 --> 02:09:59.280
The Pakistani province of Punjab
was flooded,

02:09:59.280 --> 02:10:02.760
drowning hundreds of acres
of agricultural land.

02:10:03.200 --> 02:10:05.600
Islamabad reacted angrily.

02:10:05.600 --> 02:10:09.600
It claimed this unexpected release
of water into the River Sutlej

02:10:09.600 --> 02:10:13.760
that flows from India to Pakistan
was part of an attempt by New Delhi

02:10:13.760 --> 02:10:16.240
to flout the Indus Waters Treaty.

02:10:17.840 --> 02:10:22.720
This was followed by the revocation
of Article 370 in 2019,

02:10:22.720 --> 02:10:26.840
to eliminate the special status
of Indian-administered Kashmir,

02:10:26.840 --> 02:10:29.360
an area claimed by Pakistan.

02:10:55.040 --> 02:10:59.120
As geopolitical realities
mix with a potential water war,

02:10:59.120 --> 02:11:02.440
the reaction in
Pakistan-administered Kashmir is intense.

02:11:02.440 --> 02:11:03.200
Go back!

02:11:03.200 --> 02:11:04.960
We want freedom!

02:11:04.960 --> 02:11:05.800
Go India!

02:11:05.800 --> 02:11:07.040
Go back!

02:11:07.040 --> 02:11:09.800
We travel to Pakistan-controlled Kashmir

02:11:09.800 --> 02:11:12.720
at the frontline of this complex battle

02:11:12.720 --> 02:11:15.760
where tempers are reaching boiling point.

02:12:15.720 --> 02:12:18.560
Such rallies in
Pakistan-administered Kashmir

02:12:18.560 --> 02:12:20.240
have become routine.

02:12:30.280 --> 02:12:33.720
Frequent protests are putting pressure
on the Pakistani Army

02:12:33.720 --> 02:12:36.840
to liberate Indian-administered Kashmir.

02:12:36.840 --> 02:12:40.800
And secure the supply of water
for mainland Pakistan as well.

02:13:19.560 --> 02:13:22.360
The government of
Pakistan-administered Kashmir

02:13:22.360 --> 02:13:26.560
fears that Prime Minister Modi
may usurp the Indus Waters Treaty

02:13:26.560 --> 02:13:30.040
and leave Pakistan
in a drought-like situation.

02:13:32.600 --> 02:13:36.640
In a never-ending conflict
between two nuclear armed countries,

02:13:36.640 --> 02:13:40.280
more than 50,000 Kashmiris
have lost their lives.

02:13:40.920 --> 02:13:43.520
Will the water dispute claim more?

02:13:50.960 --> 02:13:52.120
After the break,

02:13:52.120 --> 02:13:55.760
we look at Prime Minister
Imran Khan's Billion Tree Tsunami,

02:13:55.760 --> 02:13:59.840
that's being billed as the idea
that could save the region.

02:14:05.080 --> 02:14:07.280
December, 2019.

02:14:07.280 --> 02:14:10.560
New Delhi is in the grips
of an intense smog.

02:14:11.280 --> 02:14:15.000
The air quality index
reads as high as 1,000.

02:14:15.840 --> 02:14:20.000
The smog transcends the border
over to parts of Pakistan.

02:14:20.920 --> 02:14:25.280
A blame game erupts between
politicians on either side of the border.

02:14:55.200 --> 02:14:59.000
NASA satellite data shows
a heavy concentration of fires

02:14:59.000 --> 02:15:00.600
on the Indian side

02:15:00.600 --> 02:15:01.720
and far fewer

02:15:01.720 --> 02:15:03.880
on the Pakistani side of the border.

02:15:04.640 --> 02:15:06.840
The end result was a deadly smog

02:15:06.840 --> 02:15:08.600
and downgrading of air quality

02:15:08.600 --> 02:15:10.160
across the subcontinent,

02:15:10.160 --> 02:15:11.760
particularly in India.

02:15:14.440 --> 02:15:18.320
Cricket star-turned-Prime Minister
Imran Khan's surprising response

02:15:18.320 --> 02:15:19.880
grabbed headlines.

02:15:21.320 --> 02:15:24.360
His party, the Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf

02:15:24.360 --> 02:15:27.840
spearheaded Billion Tree Tsunami in 2014

02:15:27.840 --> 02:15:31.320
at a cost $169 million.

02:15:31.800 --> 02:15:35.040
This was the Pakistani government's
response to the challenge

02:15:35.040 --> 02:15:37.280
of global warming in South Asia.

02:15:37.840 --> 02:15:40.880
Pakistan has the lowest tree cover
in the region,

02:15:40.880 --> 02:15:43.240
giving it more reason to reforest.

02:15:43.240 --> 02:15:47.720
A billion trees were planted
from 2014 to 2018.

02:15:48.520 --> 02:15:51.520
But Prime Minister Khan
isn't stopping here.

02:15:51.520 --> 02:15:56.640
He has vowed to plant 10 billion trees
in the next 5 years of his government.

02:15:57.880 --> 02:15:59.720
10 Billion Tree Tsunami,

02:15:59.720 --> 02:16:02.760
but before that,
it was actually Billion Tree Tsunami.

02:16:02.760 --> 02:16:04.680
It was started by
Prime Minister Imran Khan.

02:16:04.680 --> 02:16:07.000
At that time,
he was not the prime minister.

02:16:07.000 --> 02:16:11.000
And we had one province government
that was called KPK.

02:16:11.000 --> 02:16:16.280
This was the first green initiative
that our country started in 73 years.

02:16:16.280 --> 02:16:22.680
It was one of the different
and finest projects

02:16:22.680 --> 02:16:26.240
that any government
on the provincial level started.

02:16:26.240 --> 02:16:28.040
It was known as
Billion Tree Tsunami,

02:16:28.480 --> 02:16:33.520
and it has made a mark
in history and the world.

02:16:35.640 --> 02:16:38.560
The plan is to increase
the forest cover in the country

02:16:38.560 --> 02:16:42.040
and attract rains
that will reduce rising temperatures.

02:16:42.520 --> 02:16:43.520
In total,

02:16:43.520 --> 02:16:48.640
Pakistan has restored 350,000 hectares
of forests and degraded land

02:16:48.640 --> 02:16:51.560
to surpass its
Bonn Challenge commitment.

02:16:54.960 --> 02:16:57.280
Pakistan's Vision 2025

02:16:57.280 --> 02:17:00.760
is aiming to work on better
water management as well.

02:17:01.200 --> 02:17:03.480
Due to traditional farming techniques,

02:17:03.480 --> 02:17:05.880
two-thirds of irrigation water is lost

02:17:05.920 --> 02:17:08.640
through system leakages
across the country.

02:17:10.160 --> 02:17:13.480
That's why farmers like Ashiq Bangash
have stepped in.

02:17:13.480 --> 02:17:15.120
A resident of Islamabad,

02:17:15.120 --> 02:17:18.000
Bangash decided to introduce
drip irrigation

02:17:18.000 --> 02:17:21.000
into Pakistan's traditional
farming system.

02:17:55.320 --> 02:18:00.800
Pakistan is among the six countries that
will be most affected by global warming.

02:18:00.800 --> 02:18:05.960
And a rising population calls for
measures to protect natural resources.

02:18:45.960 --> 02:18:49.840
2025 has been marked
as the year when Pakistan

02:18:49.840 --> 02:18:52.720
may turn into a "water-scarce" country,

02:18:52.720 --> 02:18:55.280
unable to meet its water needs.

02:18:59.960 --> 02:19:02.560
Existing reservoirs' storage capacity

02:19:02.600 --> 02:19:06.040
cannot sustain
Pakistan's population boom

02:19:06.040 --> 02:19:09.680
while river flow has also
been reduced over the years.

02:19:10.840 --> 02:19:15.040
Pakistan can get more economic,
social and environmental benefits

02:19:15.040 --> 02:19:16.840
from better water use.

02:19:18.520 --> 02:19:23.000
But it's subject to continuous reforms
to improve water use efficiency

02:19:23.000 --> 02:19:24.440
and service delivery,

02:19:24.440 --> 02:19:27.720
and create awareness
among the general public.

02:19:37.680 --> 02:19:38.720
In Lahore,

02:19:38.720 --> 02:19:41.280
Pakistanis are rising to the challenge

02:19:41.280 --> 02:19:45.560
to put pressure on politicians
across Pakistan to do more.

02:19:47.920 --> 02:19:50.080
People don't understand how bad it is.

02:19:50.080 --> 02:19:54.080
Just this year there were floods,
unexpected rains in Punjab

02:19:54.080 --> 02:19:56.880
that wiped out a substantial portion
of the wheat crop,

02:19:56.880 --> 02:19:58.880
that affected people's livelihoods.

02:19:58.920 --> 02:20:03.080
And for a country where a quarter of the
population is at or near the poverty line,

02:20:03.080 --> 02:20:05.200
a single event like that, one rain,

02:20:05.200 --> 02:20:08.960
is the difference between
getting a square meal a day or not.

02:20:11.160 --> 02:20:13.200
In a country beset with problems,

02:20:13.200 --> 02:20:16.920
only 4% think that climate change
is an issue.

02:20:19.920 --> 02:20:22.560
Rafay wants to change this attitude.

02:20:23.280 --> 02:20:25.960
He's taking his family
to the Climate March

02:20:25.960 --> 02:20:29.160
to raise awareness
amongst kids in Pakistan.

02:20:34.000 --> 02:20:35.520
Will you be leading the march?

02:20:35.520 --> 02:20:37.240
No, we're going to help organise it.

02:20:37.240 --> 02:20:41.400
The children are leading the march,
the students, not us.

02:20:44.680 --> 02:20:45.760
It's safer.

02:20:46.480 --> 02:20:47.800
Paper and pen.

02:20:51.000 --> 02:20:56.960
All right, so we've got a march
in Mianwali currently underway.

02:21:00.440 --> 02:21:03.640
The youth of Pakistan
are concerned about their future

02:21:03.640 --> 02:21:06.520
when the impact of climate change
may worsen.

02:21:54.080 --> 02:21:57.280
More than 5,000 marches
take place worldwide

02:21:57.280 --> 02:21:59.800
along with Lahore on the same day.

02:22:00.640 --> 02:22:05.240
The Climate Strike is the largest ever
collective call to action in the world.

02:22:07.000 --> 02:22:10.440
My government is the
biggest polluter in history

02:22:10.440 --> 02:22:12.520
and should be doing something about it

02:22:12.520 --> 02:22:16.600
but instead President Trump
doesn't even believe in climate change

02:22:16.600 --> 02:22:18.920
and wants to pull us out
of the Paris Agreement.

02:22:18.920 --> 02:22:21.000
I find it completely unacceptable,

02:22:21.000 --> 02:22:24.240
especially because my friends
and in-laws here in Pakistan

02:22:24.240 --> 02:22:27.160
are the ones who
are already suffering the most

02:22:27.160 --> 02:22:28.640
from the impact of climate change.

02:22:28.640 --> 02:22:32.920
We want justice!

02:22:59.880 --> 02:23:04.680
Pakistan contributes less than 1%
of the world's greenhouse gases.

02:23:04.680 --> 02:23:08.000
Yet, it is the world's
sixth most vulnerable country

02:23:08.000 --> 02:23:10.120
affected by climate change.

02:23:11.680 --> 02:23:14.360
While it must develop
a robust foreign policy

02:23:14.360 --> 02:23:18.920
that allows it to effectively negotiate
climate issues with its neighbours,

02:23:18.920 --> 02:23:23.120
Pakistan's march to solve
its climate problems has begun.

